to cover the planet", "body": "Geostationary: a few large satellites far away. Low orbit: many satellites close by, each in view for minutes.", "bullets": ["Iridium covers the globe with 66 satellites at about 780 km."], "sources": [{"url": "https://aerospace.csis.org/?p=22", "label": "CSIS Aerospace Security, popular orbits"}]}, {"kind": "numberSense", "id": "ns-delay", "prompt": "A signal travels about 71,600 km up to a geostationary satellite and back down. At 300,000 km per second, how many milliseconds does that take?", "min": 0, "max": 1000, "value": 240, "unit": "ms", "explanation": "71,600 ÷ 300,000 is about 0.24 seconds, or 240 ms, before any processing. Low orbit is dozens of times closer."}, {"kind": "faceOff", "id": "face", "prompt": "Which network needs more satellites to serve the same customers?", "left": {"name": "Geostationary network", "note": "Satellites at 35,786 km"}, "right": {"name": "Low-orbit network", "note": "Satellites below 2,000 km"}, "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Low satellites each see a small patch and pass quickly, so you need many more of them."}, {"id": "lens-curiosity", "kind": "info", "title": "Distance costs time", "body": "Even at light speed, a trip to 35,786 km and back takes about a quarter of a second.", "eyebrow": "Why it matters", "sources": [{"url": "https://aerospace.csis.org/?p=22", "label": "CSIS Aerospace Security, popular orbits"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In space, closer satellites are faster, but you need far more of them.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://aerospace.csis.org/?p=22", "label": "CSIS Aerospace Security, popular orbits"}, {"url": "https://teslanorth.com/2026/01/25/starlink-satellites-made-300000-moves-to-avoid-collisions-in-2025", "label": "TeslaNorth, citing SpaceX filing to the FCC"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "A geostationary satellite covers a third of Earth but a signal needs about a quarter second to go up and back. Low orbit is fast but needs thousands of satellites, like Starlink's roughly 9,400.", "sectionKey": "chain"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$b6e8230d-b715-5ee0-9dde-fb5a52be65e5$ml$::uuid, $ml$spacetech$ml$, $ml$On a rideshare, the rocket picks your orbit$ml$, 'LESSON', ARRAY[$ml$MICRO_LESSON$ml$, $ml$day-9$ml$, $ml$month-1$ml$, $ml$handwritten-v1$ml$, $ml$goal:career$ml$, $ml$level:beginner$ml$, $ml$level:intermediate$ml$, $ml$level:advanced$ml$]::text[], 5, now(), '{}'::jsonb, $ml${"id": "spacetech-d9-career", "title": "On a rideshare, the rocket picks your orbit", "exercises": [{"id": "open", "kind": "coldOpen", "kicker": "Cheap seats come with strings.", "eyebrow": "Day 9 · The power", "headline": "A 50 kg seat on SpaceX's Transporter costs $350,000 for 2026 flights.", "fullText": "SpaceX's Transporter flights carry dozens of small satellites at once to sun-synchronous orbit. The price works out to about $7,000 per kg, up from $5,500 per kg before 2023. In exchange, operators give up control over their orbital destination and their launch schedule.", "sources": [{"url": "https://newspaceeconomy.ca/2026/02/27/satellite-ridesharing-market-analysis-2026/", "label": "New Space Economy, rideshare market 2026"}, {"url": "https://payloadspace.com/spacex-increases-rideshare-prices", "label": "Payload, SpaceX raises rideshare prices"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "who-decides", "eyebrow": "Who decides", "text": "The rocket sets the orbit and the date. Rideshare payloads go along.", "highlight": "A standard Transporter won't drop you at 37 degrees or 450 km.", "keyTerms": [{"term": "Rideshare", "definition": "Many satellites sharing one rocket, splitting the cost."}], "sources": [{"url": "https://newspaceeconomy.ca/2026/02/27/satellite-ridesharing-market-analysis-2026/", "label": "New Space Economy, rideshare market 2026"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "price", "eyebrow": "Who sets the price", "text": "The rocket owner sets the price: it rose from $5,500 to $6,500 per kg in 2023.", "highlight": "It reached about $7,000 per kg for 2026 flights.", "sources": [{"url": "https://payloadspace.com/spacex-increases-rideshare-prices", "label": "Payload, SpaceX raises rideshare prices"}, {"url": "https://newspaceeconomy.ca/2026/02/27/satellite-ridesharing-market-analysis-2026/", "label": "New Space Economy, rideshare market 2026"}]}, {"kind": "info", "id": "tugs", "eyebrow": "The last mile", "title": "Space tugs sell the last mile", "body": "Orbital transfer vehicles ride up as one payload, then use their own engines to drop satellites where they need to be.", "bullets": ["D-Orbit has flown 21 of its ION carriers."], "sources": [{"url": "https://newspaceeconomy.ca/2026/02/27/satellite-ridesharing-market-analysis-2026/", "label": "New Space Economy, rideshare market 2026"}]}, {"kind": "multipleChoice", "id": "mc", "prompt": "What does a rideshare customer give up for the low price?", "options": ["The right to insure the satellite", "Control over its orbit and launch date", "Its claim on the radio spectrum"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The rocket goes where its flight plan says, on its date. Riders adapt."}, {"kind": "theCall", "id": "call", "situation": "Your satellite needs a lower altitude than the rideshare drop-off.", "prompt": "How do you get there?", "options": ["Accept the drop-off and lose image quality", "Buy a whole rocket for one small satellite", "Add a space tug or onboard thrust to move it"], "consequences": ["Your product would be worse for its whole life.", "A dedicated launch can cost many times the rideshare seat.", "Tugs exist to close the gap between the ride and your orbit."], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Tugs and thrusters close the gap between where the ride goes and where you need to be."}, {"id": "lens-career", "kind": "info", "title": "Ask where the ride drops you", "body": "Ask how a team gets from the rideshare drop-off to its working orbit. It shows you know the real constraint.", "eyebrow": "For your interview", "sources": [{"url": "https://newspaceeconomy.ca/2026/02/27/satellite-ridesharing-market-analysis-2026/", "label": "New Space Economy, rideshare market 2026"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In space, whoever owns the ride decides the orbit and the date.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://newspaceeconomy.ca/2026/02/27/satellite-ridesharing-market-analysis-2026/", "label": "New Space Economy, rideshare market 2026"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "Rideshare costs about $7,000 per kg, but the rocket picks the orbit and the date. Space tugs sell the last mile.", "sectionKey": "questions"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
