pace.com/spacex-increases-rideshare-prices", "label": "Payload, SpaceX raises rideshare prices"}, {"url": "https://newspaceeconomy.ca/2026/02/27/satellite-ridesharing-market-analysis-2026/", "label": "New Space Economy, rideshare market 2026"}]}, {"kind": "info", "id": "tugs", "eyebrow": "The last mile", "title": "Space tugs sell the last mile", "body": "Orbital transfer vehicles ride up as one payload, then use their own engines to drop satellites where they need to be.", "bullets": ["D-Orbit has flown 21 of its ION carriers."], "sources": [{"url": "https://newspaceeconomy.ca/2026/02/27/satellite-ridesharing-market-analysis-2026/", "label": "New Space Economy, rideshare market 2026"}]}, {"kind": "multipleChoice", "id": "mc", "prompt": "What does a rideshare customer give up for the low price?", "options": ["The right to insure the satellite", "Control over its orbit and launch date", "Its claim on the radio spectrum"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The rocket goes where its flight plan says, on its date. Riders adapt."}, {"kind": "theCall", "id": "call", "situation": "Your satellite needs a lower altitude than the rideshare drop-off.", "prompt": "How do you get there?", "options": ["Accept the drop-off and lose image quality", "Buy a whole rocket for one small satellite", "Add a space tug or onboard thrust to move it"], "consequences": ["Your product would be worse for its whole life.", "A dedicated launch can cost many times the rideshare seat.", "Tugs exist to close the gap between the ride and your orbit."], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Tugs and thrusters close the gap between where the ride goes and where you need to be."}, {"id": "lens-build_startup", "kind": "info", "title": "The last mile is a business", "body": "Rideshare drops everyone in roughly the same orbit. Moving satellites from there is a service people pay for.", "eyebrow": "For founders", "sources": [{"url": "https://newspaceeconomy.ca/2026/02/27/satellite-ridesharing-market-analysis-2026/", "label": "New Space Economy, rideshare market 2026"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In space, whoever owns the ride decides the orbit and the date.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://newspaceeconomy.ca/2026/02/27/satellite-ridesharing-market-analysis-2026/", "label": "New Space Economy, rideshare market 2026"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "Rideshare costs about $7,000 per kg, but the rocket picks the orbit and the date. Space tugs sell the last mile.", "sectionKey": "who_hurts"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$2caab2b2-5efa-5beb-97ef-7bd1878265fe$ml$::uuid, $ml$spacetech$ml$, $ml$On a rideshare, the rocket picks your orbit$ml$, 'LESSON', ARRAY[$ml$MICRO_LESSON$ml$, $ml$day-9$ml$, $ml$month-1$ml$, $ml$handwritten-v1$ml$, $ml$goal:curiosity$ml$, $ml$level:beginner$ml$, $ml$level:intermediate$ml$, $ml$level:advanced$ml$]::text[], 5, now(), '{}'::jsonb, $ml${"id": "spacetech-d9-curiosity", "title": "On a rideshare, the rocket picks your orbit", "exercises": [{"id": "open", "kind": "coldOpen", "kicker": "Cheap seats come with strings.", "eyebrow": "Day 9 · The power", "headline": "A 50 kg seat on SpaceX's Transporter costs $350,000 for 2026 flights.", "fullText": "SpaceX's Transporter flights carry dozens of small satellites at once to sun-synchronous orbit. The price works out to about $7,000 per kg, up from $5,500 per kg before 2023. In exchange, operators give up control over their orbital destination and their launch schedule.", "sources": [{"url": "https://newspaceeconomy.ca/2026/02/27/satellite-ridesharing-market-analysis-2026/", "label": "New Space Economy, rideshare market 2026"}, {"url": "https://payloadspace.com/spacex-increases-rideshare-prices", "label": "Payload, SpaceX raises rideshare prices"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "who-decides", "eyebrow": "Who decides", "text": "The rocket sets the orbit and the date. Rideshare payloads go along.", "highlight": "A standard Transporter won't drop you at 37 degrees or 450 km.", "keyTerms": [{"term": "Rideshare", "definition": "Many satellites sharing one rocket, splitting the cost."}], "sources": [{"url": "https://newspaceeconomy.ca/2026/02/27/satellite-ridesharing-market-analysis-2026/", "label": "New Space Economy, rideshare market 2026"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "price", "eyebrow": "Who sets the price", "text": "The rocket owner sets the price: it rose from $5,500 to $6,500 per kg in 2023.", "highlight": "It reached about $7,000 per kg for 2026 flights.", "sources": [{"url": "https://payloadspace.com/spacex-increases-rideshare-prices", "label": "Payload, SpaceX raises rideshare prices"}, {"url": "https://newspaceeconomy.ca/2026/02/27/satellite-ridesharing-market-analysis-2026/", "label": "New Space Economy, rideshare market 2026"}]}, {"kind": "info", "id": "tugs", "eyebrow": "The last mile", "title": "Space tugs sell the last mile", "body": "Orbital transfer vehicles ride up as one payload, then use their own engines to drop satellites where they need to be.", "bullets": ["D-Orbit has flown 21 of its ION carriers."], "sources": [{"url": "https://newspaceeconomy.ca/2026/02/27/satellite-ridesharing-market-analysis-2026/", "label": "New Space Economy, rideshare market 2026"}]}, {"kind": "multipleChoice", "id": "mc", "prompt": "What does a rideshare customer give up for the low price?", "options": ["The right to insure the satellite", "Control over its orbit and launch date", "Its claim on the radio spectrum"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The rocket goes where its flight plan says, on its date. Riders adapt."}, {"kind": "theCall", "id": "call", "situation": "Your satellite needs a lower altitude than the rideshare drop-off.", "prompt": "How do you get there?", "options": ["Accept the drop-off and lose image quality", "Buy a whole rocket for one small satellite", "Add a space tug or onboard thrust to move it"], "consequences": ["Your product would be worse for its whole life.", "A dedicated launch can cost many times the rideshare seat.", "Tugs exist to close the gap between the ride and your orbit."], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Tugs and thrusters close the gap between where the ride goes and where you need to be."}, {"id": "lens-curiosity", "kind": "info", "title": "Carpooling to orbit", "body": "Dozens of small satellites share one rocket, but the rocket's flight plan decides where they all go.", "eyebrow": "Why it matters", "sources": [{"url": "https://newspaceeconomy.ca/2026/02/27/satellite-ridesharing-market-analysis-2026/", "label": "New Space Economy, rideshare market 2026"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In space, whoever owns the ride decides the orbit and the date.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://newspaceeconomy.ca/2026/02/27/satellite-ridesharing-market-analysis-2026/", "label": "New Space Economy, rideshare market 2026"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "Rideshare costs about $7,000 per kg, but the rocket picks the orbit and the date. Space tugs sell the last mile.", "sectionKey": "players"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$03cb49a3-a3ae-52ef-824a-a5cbbdda036c$ml$::uuid, $ml$spacetech$ml$, $ml$How often you can see the same spot$ml$, 'LESSON', ARRAY[$ml$MICRO_LESSON$ml$, $ml$day-10$ml$, $ml$month-1$ml$, $ml$handwritten-v1$ml$, $ml$goal:career$ml$, $ml$level:beginner$ml$, $ml$level:intermediate$ml$, $ml$level:advanced$ml$]::text[], 5, now(), '{}'::jsonb, $ml${"id": "spacetech-d10-career", "title": "How often you can see the same spot", "exercises": [{"id": "open", "kind": "coldOpen", "kicker": "Imaging companies sell time, not just pictures.", "eyebrow": "Day 10 · The numbers", "headline": "Planet's 200-plus satellites image all of Earth's land every day.", "fullText": "Planet operates more than 200 small satellites that photograph Earth's entire land surface daily, at about 3.7 m per pixel. Maxar's six WorldView Legion satellites capture far sharper 30 cm images and can return to some places up to 15 times a day. How often a fleet comes back over a spot is called revisit.", "sources": [{"url": "https://learn.planet.com/rs/997-CHH-265/images/Planet Overview Datasheet.pdf", "label": "Planet overview datasheet"}, {"url": "https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20250204832232/en", "label": "Maxar, fifth and sixth WorldView Legion satellites"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "revisit", "eyebrow": "The metric", "text": "Revisit is how often a satellite fleet sees the same place.", "highlight": "Daily, hourly or 15 times a day: that number decides what change you can watch.", "keyTerms": [{"term": "Revisit", "definition": "How often a satellite or fleet passes over the same spot."}], "sources": [{"url": "https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20250204832232/en", "label": "Maxar, fifth and sixth WorldView Legion satellites"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "trade", "eyebrow": "The trade", "text": "Detail and frequency pull against each other.", "highlight": "Planet gets daily global coverage at 3.7 m; Maxar gets 30 cm detail.", "sources": [{"url": "https://learn.planet.com/rs/997-CHH-265/images/Planet Overview Datasheet.pdf", "label": "Planet overview datasheet"}, {"url": "https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20250204832232/en", "label": "Maxar, fifth and sixth WorldView Legion satellites"}]}, {"kind": "info", "id": "why-low", "eyebrow": "Why low orbit helps", "title": "More low satellites, more passes", "body": "A low satellite laps Earth every 90 minutes to 2 hours. Add satellites and each spot gets passed more often.", "sources": [{"url": "https://aerospace.csis.org/?p=22", "label": "CSIS Aerospace Security, popular orbits"}]}, {"kind": "numberSense", "id": "ns-revisit", "prompt": "Maxar's WorldView Legion fleet can revisit some places up to how many times a day?", "min": 0, "max": 40, "value": 15, "unit": "times", "explanation": "Up to 15 times a day, with six satellites."}, {"kind": "multipleChoice", "id": "mc", "prompt": "A crop insurer wants a fresh image of every farm on Earth each day. Which offer fits best?", "options": ["Six sharp satellites aimed at chosen sites", "One geostationary weather satellite", "About 200 satellites imaging all land daily"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Daily coverage of all land matters more here than 30 cm detail on a few sites."}, {"id": "lens-career", "kind": "info", "title": "Quote revisit with resolution", "body": "In imaging, give both numbers: how sharp and how often. Planet is 3.7 m daily; Maxar is 30 cm, up to 15 times a day.", "eyebrow": "For your interview", "sources": [{"url": "https://learn.planet.com/rs/997-CHH-265/images/Planet Overview Datasheet.pdf", "label": "Planet overview datasheet"}, {"url": "https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20250204832232/en", "label": "Maxar, fifth and sixth WorldView Legion satellites"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In space imaging, how often you see a place matters as much as how sharply.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://learn.planet.com/rs/997-CHH-265/images/Planet Overview Datasheet.pdf", "label": "Planet overview datasheet"}, {"url": "https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20250204832232/en", "label": "Maxar, fifth and sixth WorldView Legion satellites"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "Revisit is how often a fleet sees the same spot. Planet images all land daily at 3.7 m; Maxar gets 30 cm detail up to 15 times a day.", "sectionKey": "numbers"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
