 that pushes the payload the rest of the way to orbit."}], "sources": [{"url": "https://spacedaily.com/t-we-tend-to-think-reusable-rockets-are-still-experimental-but-in-2025-spacex-s-partly-reusable-falcon-9-flew-165-missions-one-every-2-2-days-and-successfully-delivered-its-payload-on-every-flight/", "label": "Space Daily, Falcon 9 in 2025"}]}, {"kind": "info", "id": "routine", "eyebrow": "The record", "title": "Reuse is now routine", "body": "Falcon 9 had flown about 620 times by March 2026, with a mission success rate above 99%.", "bullets": ["All 165 of its 2025 launches delivered their payloads."], "sources": [{"url": "https://spacedaily.com/t-we-tend-to-think-reusable-rockets-are-still-experimental-but-in-2025-spacex-s-partly-reusable-falcon-9-flew-165-missions-one-every-2-2-days-and-successfully-delivered-its-payload-on-every-flight/", "label": "Space Daily, Falcon 9 in 2025"}]}, {"kind": "sortSignal", "id": "sort", "prompt": "On a Falcon 9, reused or thrown away?", "buckets": ["Usually reused", "Thrown away"], "items": [{"text": "First-stage booster", "bucket": 0}, {"text": "Payload fairing halves", "bucket": 0}, {"text": "Second stage", "bucket": 1}, {"text": "Second-stage engine", "bucket": 1}], "explanation": "The booster and often the fairings fly again. The second stage, engine included, is expended."}, {"kind": "multipleChoice", "id": "mc", "prompt": "Why is the second stage the hardest part to bring back?", "options": ["It is the most expensive part of the rocket", "It reaches orbit, so it has no easy way home", "Regulators do not allow anyone to reuse it"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The booster turns around before orbit. The second stage ends up in orbit, far from the pad and moving fast."}, {"id": "lens-invest", "kind": "info", "title": "Reuse changes the cost base", "body": "When 157 of 165 launches use flown boosters, most hardware is not rebuilt each time. Ask what rivals still throw away.", "eyebrow": "For investors", "sources": [{"url": "https://spacedaily.com/t-we-tend-to-think-reusable-rockets-are-still-experimental-but-in-2025-spacex-s-partly-reusable-falcon-9-flew-165-missions-one-every-2-2-days-and-successfully-delivered-its-payload-on-every-flight/", "label": "Space Daily, Falcon 9 in 2025"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In launch, whatever you throw away sets your cost floor.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://spacedaily.com/t-we-tend-to-think-reusable-rockets-are-still-experimental-but-in-2025-spacex-s-partly-reusable-falcon-9-flew-165-missions-one-every-2-2-days-and-successfully-delivered-its-payload-on-every-flight/", "label": "Space Daily, Falcon 9 in 2025"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "Falcon 9 reuses its booster and often its fairings, but throws away the second stage. In 2025, 157 of 165 launches used boosters that had flown before.", "sectionKey": "watchlist"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$3e728808-0b70-5007-af74-34c2e81e4936$ml$::uuid, $ml$spacetech$ml$, $ml$What reuse really reuses$ml$, 'LESSON', ARRAY[$ml$MICRO_LESSON$ml$, $ml$day-13$ml$, $ml$month-1$ml$, $ml$handwritten-v1$ml$, $ml$goal:build_startup$ml$, $ml$level:beginner$ml$, $ml$level:intermediate$ml$, $ml$level:advanced$ml$]::text[], 5, now(), '{}'::jsonb, $ml${"id": "spacetech-d13-build_startup", "title": "What reuse really reuses", "exercises": [{"id": "open", "kind": "coldOpen", "kicker": "Most of a Falcon 9 comes home.", "eyebrow": "Day 13 · Reuse", "headline": "In 2025, 157 of 165 Falcon 9 launches flew on boosters that had flown before.", "fullText": "The first stage flies back, lands and is used again. Payload fairing halves are recovered and reflown on many missions. The second stage, which pushes the payload into orbit, is thrown away every time. In 2025 boosters came back on all but three of the 165 flights.", "sources": [{"url": "https://spacedaily.com/t-we-tend-to-think-reusable-rockets-are-still-experimental-but-in-2025-spacex-s-partly-reusable-falcon-9-flew-165-missions-one-every-2-2-days-and-successfully-delivered-its-payload-on-every-flight/", "label": "Space Daily, Falcon 9 in 2025"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "booster", "eyebrow": "What comes back", "text": "The booster is the biggest part of the rocket, and it lands.", "highlight": "In 2025 boosters returned on all but three missions, two of them expended on purpose.", "sources": [{"url": "https://spacedaily.com/t-we-tend-to-think-reusable-rockets-are-still-experimental-but-in-2025-spacex-s-partly-reusable-falcon-9-flew-165-missions-one-every-2-2-days-and-successfully-delivered-its-payload-on-every-flight/", "label": "Space Daily, Falcon 9 in 2025"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "upper", "eyebrow": "What doesn't", "text": "The second stage is lost on every flight.", "highlight": "It is the part that reaches orbit, so it has no easy way home.", "keyTerms": [{"term": "Second stage", "definition": "The upper part of a rocket that pushes the payload the rest of the way to orbit."}], "sources": [{"url": "https://spacedaily.com/t-we-tend-to-think-reusable-rockets-are-still-experimental-but-in-2025-spacex-s-partly-reusable-falcon-9-flew-165-missions-one-every-2-2-days-and-successfully-delivered-its-payload-on-every-flight/", "label": "Space Daily, Falcon 9 in 2025"}]}, {"kind": "info", "id": "routine", "eyebrow": "The record", "title": "Reuse is now routine", "body": "Falcon 9 had flown about 620 times by March 2026, with a mission success rate above 99%.", "bullets": ["All 165 of its 2025 launches delivered their payloads."], "sources": [{"url": "https://spacedaily.com/t-we-tend-to-think-reusable-rockets-are-still-experimental-but-in-2025-spacex-s-partly-reusable-falcon-9-flew-165-missions-one-every-2-2-days-and-successfully-delivered-its-payload-on-every-flight/", "label": "Space Daily, Falcon 9 in 2025"}]}, {"kind": "sortSignal", "id": "sort", "prompt": "On a Falcon 9, reused or thrown away?", "buckets": ["Usually reused", "Thrown away"], "items": [{"text": "First-stage booster", "bucket": 0}, {"text": "Payload fairing halves", "bucket": 0}, {"text": "Second stage", "bucket": 1}, {"text": "Second-stage engine", "bucket": 1}], "explanation": "The booster and often the fairings fly again. The second stage, engine included, is expended."}, {"kind": "multipleChoice", "id": "mc", "prompt": "Why is the second stage the hardest part to bring back?", "options": ["It is the most expensive part of the rocket", "It reaches orbit, so it has no easy way home", "Regulators do not allow anyone to reuse it"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The booster turns around before orbit. The second stage ends up in orbit, far from the pad and moving fast."}, {"id": "lens-build_startup", "kind": "info", "title": "The next part to bring home", "body": "The second stage is still thrown away on every Falcon 9. Whoever brings it back cuts cost again.", "eyebrow": "For founders", "sources": [{"url": "https://spacedaily.com/t-we-tend-to-think-reusable-rockets-are-still-experimental-but-in-2025-spacex-s-partly-reusable-falcon-9-flew-165-missions-one-every-2-2-days-and-successfully-delivered-its-payload-on-every-flight/", "label": "Space Daily, Falcon 9 in 2025"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In launch, whatever you throw away sets your cost floor.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://spacedaily.com/t-we-tend-to-think-reusable-rockets-are-still-experimental-but-in-2025-spacex-s-partly-reusable-falcon-9-flew-165-missions-one-every-2-2-days-and-successfully-delivered-its-payload-on-every-flight/", "label": "Space Daily, Falcon 9 in 2025"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "Falcon 9 reuses its booster and often its fairings, but throws away the second stage. In 2025, 157 of 165 launches used boosters that had flown before.", "sectionKey": "money"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$445275bb-1673-5f50-81f9-a564b2016766$ml$::uuid, $ml$spacetech$ml$, $ml$What reuse really reuses$ml$, 'LESSON', ARRAY[$ml$MICRO_LESSON$ml$, $ml$day-13$ml$, $ml$month-1$ml$, $ml$handwritten-v1$ml$, $ml$goal:curiosity$ml$, $ml$level:beginner$ml$, $ml$level:intermediate$ml$, $ml$level:advanced$ml$]::text[], 5, now(), '{}'::jsonb, $ml${"id": "spacetech-d13-curiosity", "title": "What reuse really reuses", "exercises": [{"id": "open", "kind": "coldOpen", "kicker": "Most of a Falcon 9 comes home.", "eyebrow": "Day 13 · Reuse", "headline": "In 2025, 157 of 165 Falcon 9 launches flew on boosters that had flown before.", "fullText": "The first stage flies back, lands and is used again. Payload fairing halves are recovered and reflown on many missions. The second stage, which pushes the payload into orbit, is thrown away every time. In 2025 boosters came back on all but three of the 165 flights.", "sources": [{"url": "https://spacedaily.com/t-we-tend-to-think-reusable-rockets-are-still-experimental-but-in-2025-spacex-s-partly-reusable-falcon-9-flew-165-missions-one-every-2-2-days-and-successfully-delivered-its-payload-on-every-flight/", "label": "Space Daily, Falcon 9 in 2025"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "booster", "eyebrow": "What comes back", "text": "The booster is the biggest part of the rocket, and it lands.", "highlight": "In 2025 boosters returned on all but three missions, two of them expended on purpose.", "sources": [{"url": "https://spacedaily.com/t-we-tend-to-think-reusable-rockets-are-still-experimental-but-in-2025-spacex-s-partly-reusable-falcon-9-flew-165-missions-one-every-2-2-days-and-successfully-delivered-its-payload-on-every-flight/", "label": "Space Daily, Falcon 9 in 2025"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "upper", "eyebrow": "What doesn't", "text": "The second stage is lost on every flight.", "highlight": "It is the part that reaches orbit, so it has no easy way home.", "keyTerms": [{"term": "Second stage", "definition": "The upper part of a rocket that pushes the payload the rest of the way to orbit."}], "sources": [{"url": "https://spacedaily.com/t-we-tend-to-think-reusable-rockets-are-still-experimental-but-in-2025-spacex-s-partly-reusable-falcon-9-flew-165-missions-one-every-2-2-days-and-successfully-delivered-its-payload-on-every-flight/", "label": "Space Daily, Falcon 9 in 2025"}]}, {"kind": "info", "id": "routine", "eyebrow": "The record", "title": "Reuse is now routine", "body": "Falcon 9 had flown about 620 times by March 2026, with a mission success rate above 99%.", "bullets": ["All 165 of its 2025 launches delivered their payloads."], "sources": [{"url": "https://spacedaily.com/t-we-tend-to-think-reusable-rockets-are-still-experimental-but-in-2025-spacex-s-partly-reusable-falcon-9-flew-165-missions-one-every-2-2-days-and-successfully-delivered-its-payload-on-every-flight/", "label": "Space Daily, Falcon 9 in 2025"}]}, {"kind": "sortSignal", "id": "sort", "prompt": "On a Falcon 9, reused or thrown away?", "buckets": ["Usually reused", "Thrown away"], "items": [{"text": "First-stage booster", "bucket": 0}, {"text": "Payload fairing halves", "bucket": 0}, {"text": "Second stage", "bucket": 1}, {"text": "Second-stage engine", "bucket": 1}], "explanation": "The booster and often the fairings fly again. The second stage, engine included, is expended."}, {"kind": "multipleChoice", "id": "mc", "prompt": "Why is the second stage the hardest part to bring back?", "options": ["It is the most expensive part of the rocket", "It reaches orbit, so it has no easy way home", "Regulators do not allow anyone to reuse it"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "The booster turns around before orbit. The second stage ends up in orbit, far from the pad and moving fast."}, {"id": "lens-curiosity", "kind": "info", "title": "Rockets that land", "body": "Boosters landing and flying again is now routine: it happened on almost every Falcon 9 flight in 2025.", "eyebrow": "Why it matters", "sources": [{"url": "https://spacedaily.com/t-we-tend-to-think-reusable-rockets-are-still-experimental-but-in-2025-spacex-s-partly-reusable-falcon-9-flew-165-missions-one-every-2-2-days-and-successfully-delivered-its-payload-on-every-flight/", "label": "Space Daily, Falcon 9 in 2025"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In launch, whatever you throw away sets your cost floor.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://spacedaily.com/t-we-tend-to-think-reusable-rockets-are-still-experimental-but-in-2025-spacex-s-partly-reusable-falcon-9-flew-165-missions-one-every-2-2-days-and-successfully-delivered-its-payload-on-every-flight/", "label": "Space Daily, Falcon 9 in 2025"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "Falcon 9 reuses its booster and often its fairings, but throws away the second stage. In 2025, 157 of 165 launches used boosters that had flown before.", "sectionKey": "basics"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$8d141a41-0ab3-5706-b424-aef32d6a85f7$ml$::uuid, $ml$spacetech$ml$, $ml$When satellites fail, insurers pay$ml$, 'LESSON', ARRAY[$ml$MICRO_LESSON$ml$, $ml$day-14$ml$, $ml$month-1$ml$, $ml$handwritten-v1$ml$, $ml$goal:career$ml$, $ml$level:beginner$ml$, $ml$level:intermediate$ml$, $ml$level:advanced$ml$]::text[], 5, now(), '{}'::jsonb, $ml${"id": "spacetech-d14-career", "title": "When satellites fail, insurers pay", "exercises": [{"id": "open", "kind": "coldOpen", "kicker": "One bad year wiped out the profits.", "eyebrow": "Day 14 · The money", "headline": "In 2023, space insurers paid $995 million in claims but collected only about $557 million.", "fullText": "Two satellites caused most of the damage: ViaSat-3 Americas led to a $445 million claim and Inmarsat-6 F2, after a battery failure, to $348 million. The net loss of $438 million was a record. The premium for a typical geostationary satellite on a Falcon 9 rose from under 6% of its insured value to about 10%.", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.satellitetoday.com/sustainability/2024/05/01/slingshot-aerospace-reveals-record-insurance-losses-in-2023-in-new-satellite-deployments-report", "label": "Via Satellite, record space insurance losses in 2023"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "premium", "eyebrow": "How it works", "text": "Operators pay a premium: a share of the satellite's insured value.", "highlight": "For a geostationary satellite on Falcon 9, it rose from under 6% to about 10% in 2023.", "keyTerms": [{"term": "Premium", "definition": "What an operator pays an insurer, often quoted as a percent of insured value."}], "sources": [{"url": "https://www.satellitetoday.com/sustainability/2024/05/01/slingshot-aerospace-reveals-record-insurance-losses-in-2023-in-new-satellite-deployments-report", "label": "Via Satellite, record space insurance losses in 2023"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "big-bets", "eyebrow": "The concentration", "text": "A few big satellites carry most of the risk.", "highlight": "Two claims, $445 million and $348 million, made 2023 a record loss.", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.satellitetoday.com/sustainability/2024/05/01/slingshot-aerospace-reveals-record-insurance-losses-in-2023-in-new-satellite-deployments-report", "label": "Via Satellite, record space insurance losses in 2023"}]}, {"kind": "info", "id": "exits", "eyebrow": "The aftermath", "title": "Some insurers walked away", "body": "After 2023, some insurers left the space market completely. Fewer insurers means less capacity and higher prices.", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.satellitetoday.com/sustainability/2024/05/01/slingshot-aerospace-reveals-record-insurance-losses-in-2023-in-new-satellite-deployments-report", "label": "Via Satellite, record space insurance losses in 2023"}]}, {"kind": "numberSense", "id": "ns-loss", "prompt": "Space insurers paid $995 million in 2023 claims and collected about $557 million in premiums. What was the loss, in millions of dollars?", "min": 0, "max": 1000, "value": 438, "unit": "M", "explanation": "$995 million minus $557 million is about $438 million, a record."}, {"kind": "theCall", "id": "call", "situation": "You are about to launch a $300 million geostationary satellite. Premiums just jumped to about 10%.", "prompt": "What do you do?", "options": ["Fly uninsured to save the premium", "Delay the launch until premiums fall", "Insure it and budget the higher premium"], "consequences": ["One failure could sink the company.", "Every month on the ground delays revenue, and prices may not fall.", "About $30 million protects a $300 million asset."], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "Few operators can absorb losing a $300 million satellite. The premium is the price of surviving a failure."}, {"id": "lens-career", "kind": "info", "title": "Know who carries the risk", "body": "Ask whether a satellite is insured and for how much. It shows you understand who loses money if it fails.", "eyebrow": "For your interview", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.satellitetoday.com/sustainability/2024/05/01/slingshot-aerospace-reveals-record-insurance-losses-in-2023-in-new-satellite-deployments-report", "label": "Via Satellite, record space insurance losses in 2023"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In space, insurers price the risk, and one bad year resets the price for everyone.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.satellitetoday.com/sustainability/2024/05/01/slingshot-aerospace-reveals-record-insurance-losses-in-2023-in-new-satellite-deployments-report", "label": "Via Satellite, record space insurance losses in 2023"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "In 2023, insurers paid $995 million in space claims against about $557 million in premiums. Rates for a geostationary satellite rose from under 6% to about 10%.", "sectionKey": "how_money_moves"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
