hrough 2029.", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.satellitetoday.com/launch/2025/04/06/spacex-ula-blue-origin-receive-nssl-contracts-worth-up-to-nearly-14b/", "label": "Via Satellite, NSSL Phase 3 Lane 2 awards"}]}, {"kind": "info", "id": "two-rockets", "eyebrow": "The rule behind it", "title": "Why the work is split", "body": "Congress made it policy to keep at least two launch vehicles that can carry any national security payload. If one is grounded, the other still flies.", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.everycrsreport.com/files/2026-01-14_IF12900_e1a9a9283349423491e151fac8e59426a6fa6708.html", "label": "Congressional Research Service, NSSL primer"}]}, {"kind": "faceOff", "id": "face", "prompt": "Who holds more power in this market?", "left": {"name": "The Space Force", "note": "Picks who flies its 54 hardest missions"}, "right": {"name": "One launch company", "note": "Needs those missions to keep busy"}, "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "The buyer chooses among providers. Each provider needs the buyer more than the reverse."}, {"kind": "multipleChoice", "id": "mc", "prompt": "Why didn't the Space Force give all 54 missions to one provider?", "options": ["SpaceX could not build enough rockets", "The law caps each company at ten missions", "It must keep two ways to reach orbit"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "US policy requires at least two launch vehicles that can carry any national security payload."}, {"id": "lens-curiosity", "kind": "info", "title": "Three companies, 54 missions", "body": "The US splits its hardest launches across three companies so it never depends on one rocket.", "eyebrow": "Why it matters", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.satellitetoday.com/launch/2025/04/06/spacex-ula-blue-origin-receive-nssl-contracts-worth-up-to-nearly-14b/", "label": "Via Satellite, NSSL Phase 3 Lane 2 awards"}, {"url": "https://www.everycrsreport.com/files/2026-01-14_IF12900_e1a9a9283349423491e151fac8e59426a6fa6708.html", "label": "Congressional Research Service, NSSL primer"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In launch, an anchor customer can decide who survives.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.satellitetoday.com/launch/2025/04/06/spacex-ula-blue-origin-receive-nssl-contracts-worth-up-to-nearly-14b/", "label": "Via Satellite, NSSL Phase 3 Lane 2 awards"}, {"url": "https://www.everycrsreport.com/files/2026-01-14_IF12900_e1a9a9283349423491e151fac8e59426a6fa6708.html", "label": "Congressional Research Service, NSSL primer"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "In April 2025 the Space Force split 54 national security launches between SpaceX, ULA and Blue Origin. US policy requires at least two launch vehicles, so it never depends on one rocket.", "sectionKey": "players"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$e228533e-5aa9-5bd8-8697-c2dd96334cf6$ml$::uuid, $ml$spacetech$ml$, $ml$Six of every seven kilograms$ml$, 'LESSON', ARRAY[$ml$MICRO_LESSON$ml$, $ml$day-16$ml$, $ml$month-1$ml$, $ml$handwritten-v1$ml$, $ml$goal:career$ml$, $ml$level:beginner$ml$, $ml$level:intermediate$ml$, $ml$level:advanced$ml$]::text[], 5, now(), '{}'::jsonb, $ml${"id": "spacetech-d16-career", "title": "Six of every seven kilograms", "exercises": [{"id": "open", "kind": "coldOpen", "kicker": "Count kilograms, not launches.", "eyebrow": "Day 16 · The numbers", "headline": "In the third quarter of 2024, SpaceX carried 85.7% of all mass launched to space.", "fullText": "SpaceX launched 362 metric tons of payload in those three months. Counting launches undersells how concentrated the market is: a few heavy, frequent rockets carry most of the mass. In 2025 Falcon 9 flew about once every 2.2 days.", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.newsminimalist.com/articles/spacex-launches-86percent-of-global-payload-to-space-in-third-quarter-of-2024-452b896c", "label": "SpaceX share of global upmass, Q3 2024"}, {"url": "https://spacedaily.com/t-we-tend-to-think-reusable-rockets-are-still-experimental-but-in-2025-spacex-s-partly-reusable-falcon-9-flew-165-missions-one-every-2-2-days-and-successfully-delivered-its-payload-on-every-flight/", "label": "Space Daily, Falcon 9 in 2025"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "upmass", "eyebrow": "The metric", "text": "Upmass is the total weight a company sends to orbit.", "highlight": "It shows who really carries the market.", "keyTerms": [{"term": "Upmass", "definition": "The total mass of payloads launched to space in a period."}], "sources": [{"url": "https://www.newsminimalist.com/articles/spacex-launches-86percent-of-global-payload-to-space-in-third-quarter-of-2024-452b896c", "label": "SpaceX share of global upmass, Q3 2024"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "cadence", "eyebrow": "The pace", "text": "Cadence is how often a rocket flies.", "highlight": "Falcon 9 flew 165 times in 2025, about once every 2.2 days.", "keyTerms": [{"term": "Cadence", "definition": "How often a rocket launches."}], "sources": [{"url": "https://spacedaily.com/t-we-tend-to-think-reusable-rockets-are-still-experimental-but-in-2025-spacex-s-partly-reusable-falcon-9-flew-165-missions-one-every-2-2-days-and-successfully-delivered-its-payload-on-every-flight/", "label": "Space Daily, Falcon 9 in 2025"}]}, {"kind": "info", "id": "mislead", "eyebrow": "Read it right", "title": "Launch counts can mislead", "body": "A small rocket and a heavy one each count as one launch. Mass shows who carries the cargo.", "bullets": ["362 metric tons went up on SpaceX rockets in one quarter."], "sources": [{"url": "https://www.newsminimalist.com/articles/spacex-launches-86percent-of-global-payload-to-space-in-third-quarter-of-2024-452b896c", "label": "SpaceX share of global upmass, Q3 2024"}]}, {"kind": "numberSense", "id": "ns-share", "prompt": "What share of all mass launched to space did SpaceX carry in the third quarter of 2024?", "min": 0, "max": 100, "value": 86, "unit": "%", "explanation": "85.7%: 362 metric tons in three months."}, {"kind": "spotFake", "id": "fake", "prompt": "One of these is false. Tap it.", "statements": ["SpaceX launched 362 metric tons in the third quarter of 2024.", "A small rocket carries as much mass as a heavy one.", "Falcon 9 flew about once every 2.2 days in 2025."], "fakeIndex": 1, "explanation": "Each counts as one launch, but a heavy rocket carries far more mass."}, {"id": "lens-career", "kind": "info", "title": "Quote mass, not just launches", "body": "Give the share of mass, not only the launch count. 85.7% in one quarter is the figure that shows dominance.", "eyebrow": "For your interview", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.newsminimalist.com/articles/spacex-launches-86percent-of-global-payload-to-space-in-third-quarter-of-2024-452b896c", "label": "SpaceX share of global upmass, Q3 2024"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In launch, mass to orbit shows who really holds the market.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.newsminimalist.com/articles/spacex-launches-86percent-of-global-payload-to-space-in-third-quarter-of-2024-452b896c", "label": "SpaceX share of global upmass, Q3 2024"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "SpaceX carried 85.7% of all mass launched in the third quarter of 2024, 362 metric tons. Mass, not launch count, shows who carries the market.", "sectionKey": "numbers"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
