flew 165 times in 2025, about once every 2.2 days.", "keyTerms": [{"term": "Cadence", "definition": "How often a rocket launches."}], "sources": [{"url": "https://spacedaily.com/t-we-tend-to-think-reusable-rockets-are-still-experimental-but-in-2025-spacex-s-partly-reusable-falcon-9-flew-165-missions-one-every-2-2-days-and-successfully-delivered-its-payload-on-every-flight/", "label": "Space Daily, Falcon 9 in 2025"}]}, {"kind": "info", "id": "mislead", "eyebrow": "Read it right", "title": "Launch counts can mislead", "body": "A small rocket and a heavy one each count as one launch. Mass shows who carries the cargo.", "bullets": ["362 metric tons went up on SpaceX rockets in one quarter."], "sources": [{"url": "https://www.newsminimalist.com/articles/spacex-launches-86percent-of-global-payload-to-space-in-third-quarter-of-2024-452b896c", "label": "SpaceX share of global upmass, Q3 2024"}]}, {"kind": "numberSense", "id": "ns-share", "prompt": "What share of all mass launched to space did SpaceX carry in the third quarter of 2024?", "min": 0, "max": 100, "value": 86, "unit": "%", "explanation": "85.7%: 362 metric tons in three months."}, {"kind": "spotFake", "id": "fake", "prompt": "One of these is false. Tap it.", "statements": ["SpaceX launched 362 metric tons in the third quarter of 2024.", "A small rocket carries as much mass as a heavy one.", "Falcon 9 flew about once every 2.2 days in 2025."], "fakeIndex": 1, "explanation": "Each counts as one launch, but a heavy rocket carries far more mass."}, {"id": "lens-curiosity", "kind": "info", "title": "One company, most of the cargo", "body": "In late 2024, about six of every seven kilograms sent to space flew on SpaceX.", "eyebrow": "Why it matters", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.newsminimalist.com/articles/spacex-launches-86percent-of-global-payload-to-space-in-third-quarter-of-2024-452b896c", "label": "SpaceX share of global upmass, Q3 2024"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In launch, mass to orbit shows who really holds the market.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.newsminimalist.com/articles/spacex-launches-86percent-of-global-payload-to-space-in-third-quarter-of-2024-452b896c", "label": "SpaceX share of global upmass, Q3 2024"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "SpaceX carried 85.7% of all mass launched in the third quarter of 2024, 362 metric tons. Mass, not launch count, shows who carries the market.", "sectionKey": "numbers"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$f37a03aa-25de-5e6b-988f-b8ef525ef65e$ml$::uuid, $ml$spacetech$ml$, $ml$Grounded after one failure$ml$, 'LESSON', ARRAY[$ml$MICRO_LESSON$ml$, $ml$day-17$ml$, $ml$month-1$ml$, $ml$handwritten-v1$ml$, $ml$goal:career$ml$, $ml$level:beginner$ml$, $ml$level:intermediate$ml$, $ml$level:advanced$ml$]::text[], 5, now(), '{}'::jsonb, $ml${"id": "spacetech-d17-career", "title": "Grounded after one failure", "exercises": [{"id": "open", "kind": "coldOpen", "kicker": "Even a near-perfect rocket stops after a failure.", "eyebrow": "Day 17 · The rule", "headline": "On July 11, 2024, a Falcon 9 upper stage failed and 20 Starlink satellites were lost.", "fullText": "An oxygen leak developed when the second-stage engine fired a second time, leaving the satellites too low to survive. No one on the ground was hurt, but the FAA required an investigation before Falcon 9 could fly again. It was only the rocket's second in-flight failure, after 364 successful flights since 2010.", "sources": [{"url": "https://spacepolicyonline.com/news/rare-spacex-failure-leaves-launch-schedules-in-flux/", "label": "SpacePolicyOnline, rare SpaceX failure"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "faa", "eyebrow": "Who decides", "text": "After a mishap, the FAA must be satisfied that public safety is not at risk.", "highlight": "Until then, the rocket stays on the ground.", "keyTerms": [{"term": "Mishap investigation", "definition": "The review the FAA requires after a launch failure before the rocket can fly again."}], "sources": [{"url": "https://spacepolicyonline.com/news/rare-spacex-failure-leaves-launch-schedules-in-flux/", "label": "SpacePolicyOnline, rare SpaceX failure"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "cause", "eyebrow": "The cause", "text": "A cracked oxygen sense line, worn by engine vibration, caused the leak.", "highlight": "Crewed flights and NASA science missions were waiting behind it.", "sources": [{"url": "https://cosmiclog.com/2024/07/27/spacex-resumes-falcon-9-launches-after-faa-go-ahead/", "label": "Cosmic Log, Falcon 9 returns after FAA go-ahead"}, {"url": "https://spacepolicyonline.com/news/rare-spacex-failure-leaves-launch-schedules-in-flux/", "label": "SpacePolicyOnline, rare SpaceX failure"}]}, {"kind": "info", "id": "back", "eyebrow": "The return", "title": "Back in 16 days", "body": "The FAA allowed Falcon 9 to return to flight on July 25, 2024, and it launched again on July 27.", "sources": [{"url": "https://cosmiclog.com/2024/07/27/spacex-resumes-falcon-9-launches-after-faa-go-ahead/", "label": "Cosmic Log, Falcon 9 returns after FAA go-ahead"}]}, {"kind": "buildChain", "id": "chain", "prompt": "Put the steps after a launch failure in order.", "steps": ["The rocket fails in flight", "The FAA requires a mishap investigation", "The company finds the cause", "The FAA allows a return to flight"], "explanation": "Failure, investigation, cause found, then permission to fly again: 16 days for Falcon 9 in 2024."}, {"kind": "theCall", "id": "call", "situation": "Your satellite was due to launch next week on the grounded rocket.", "prompt": "What do you do?", "options": ["Move to another rocket right away", "Keep the slot and wait for the review", "Cancel the mission entirely"], "consequences": ["Moving to a new rocket can take months of integration work.", "Falcon 9 was flying again 16 days after the failure.", "Cancelling wastes the satellite and the work around it."], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "A reliable rocket's short grounding usually costs less than switching rides."}, {"id": "lens-career", "kind": "info", "title": "Know the mishap process", "body": "After a failure, the FAA must clear a return to flight. Knowing that sequence shows you understand launch operations.", "eyebrow": "For your interview", "sources": [{"url": "https://spacepolicyonline.com/news/rare-spacex-failure-leaves-launch-schedules-in-flux/", "label": "SpacePolicyOnline, rare SpaceX failure"}, {"url": "https://cosmiclog.com/2024/07/27/spacex-resumes-falcon-9-launches-after-faa-go-ahead/", "label": "Cosmic Log, Falcon 9 returns after FAA go-ahead"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In launch, safety rules can stop even the most reliable rocket.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://spacepolicyonline.com/news/rare-spacex-failure-leaves-launch-schedules-in-flux/", "label": "SpacePolicyOnline, rare SpaceX failure"}, {"url": "https://cosmiclog.com/2024/07/27/spacex-resumes-falcon-9-launches-after-faa-go-ahead/", "label": "Cosmic Log, Falcon 9 returns after FAA go-ahead"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "After a Falcon 9 upper stage failed in July 2024, the FAA required an investigation before it could fly again, 16 days later. Even a near-perfect rocket stops after a mishap.", "sectionKey": "risk"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
