yOnline, Blue Origin wins NASA's second lander"}]}, {"kind": "faceOff", "id": "face", "prompt": "After NASA picked a second lander, who gained bargaining power?", "left": {"name": "NASA", "note": "Now has two lander suppliers"}, "right": {"name": "SpaceX", "note": "No longer the only lander supplier"}, "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "A buyer with a backup can push each supplier harder."}, {"kind": "multipleChoice", "id": "mc", "prompt": "Why did NASA fund a second Moon lander?", "options": ["For redundancy and competition", "SpaceX's design failed its review", "Congress banned single bids"], "correctIndex": 0, "explanation": "NASA said it wanted two providers for redundancy and competition on long-term Moon missions."}, {"id": "lens-invest", "kind": "info", "title": "Second sources cap pricing power", "body": "When a buyer funds a rival, the first supplier's pricing power shrinks. Watch for second-source awards.", "eyebrow": "For investors", "sources": [{"url": "https://spacepolicyonline.com/?p=62197", "label": "SpacePolicyOnline, Blue Origin wins NASA's second lander"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In space exploration, paying for a second supplier buys the buyer bargaining power.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://spacepolicyonline.com/?p=62197", "label": "SpacePolicyOnline, Blue Origin wins NASA's second lander"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "NASA paid Blue Origin $3.4 billion in 2023 for a second Moon lander after SpaceX's 2021 win. Two suppliers give NASA redundancy and competition.", "sectionKey": "thesis"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
