ad it right", "title": "Read budgets as shares", "body": "A rising dollar total can still be a falling priority. Compare a budget with total federal spending.", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.statista.com/chart/amp/17504/nasas-budget-share-of-the-us-federal-budget", "label": "Statista, NASA's share of the federal budget"}]}, {"kind": "numberSense", "id": "ns-ratio", "prompt": "NASA got 4.41% of the federal budget in 1966 and 0.49% in 2019. About how many times smaller was the 2019 share?", "min": 1, "max": 20, "value": 9, "unit": "x", "explanation": "4.41 divided by 0.49 is 9."}, {"kind": "spotFake", "id": "fake", "prompt": "One of these is false. Tap it.", "statements": ["NASA's share of the federal budget peaked in 1966.", "NASA got its fiscal 2019 budget of $21.5 billion.", "NASA got about 4% of federal spending in 2019."], "fakeIndex": 2, "explanation": "In 2019 it was 0.49%, less than half a percent."}, {"id": "lens-curiosity", "kind": "info", "title": "Half a cent per dollar", "body": "Of each federal dollar, NASA gets about half a cent, against more than four cents in 1966.", "eyebrow": "Why it matters", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.statista.com/chart/amp/17504/nasas-budget-share-of-the-us-federal-budget", "label": "Statista, NASA's share of the federal budget"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In government space, a budget's share of spending shows its real priority.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.statista.com/chart/amp/17504/nasas-budget-share-of-the-us-federal-budget", "label": "Statista, NASA's share of the federal budget"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "NASA got 4.41% of the federal budget in 1966 and 0.49% in 2019. Its share, not its dollar total, shows how much priority space gets.", "sectionKey": "numbers"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$06a86127-eb9a-558e-bf99-6eb39b66e9bd$ml$::uuid, $ml$spacetech$ml$, $ml$No nation owns the Moon$ml$, 'LESSON', ARRAY[$ml$MICRO_LESSON$ml$, $ml$day-23$ml$, $ml$month-1$ml$, $ml$handwritten-v1$ml$, $ml$goal:career$ml$, $ml$level:beginner$ml$, $ml$level:intermediate$ml$, $ml$level:advanced$ml$]::text[], 5, now(), '{}'::jsonb, $ml${"id": "spacetech-d23-career", "title": "No nation owns the Moon", "exercises": [{"id": "open", "kind": "coldOpen", "kicker": "A 1967 treaty still governs the Moon.", "eyebrow": "Day 23 · The rule", "headline": "Under the 1967 Outer Space Treaty, no country can claim the Moon or any part of space.", "fullText": "Article II says outer space, the Moon and other celestial bodies are not subject to national appropriation, whether by claim of sovereignty, use, occupation or any other means. Article VI makes each country responsible for its companies' activities in space. The US-led Artemis Accords, launched in 2020 by eight nations, add practical principles on top.", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/spacelaw/activities/2020/SLC2020Presentations/SLC2020PDFPresentations/A._Newman_-_8_Dec_2020_-_Fundamentals_of_Space_Law_.pdf", "label": "UNOOSA, fundamentals of space law"}, {"url": "https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-celebrates-as-artemis-accords-surpasses-75-signatories/", "label": "NASA, Artemis Accords pass 75 signatories"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "no-claims", "eyebrow": "The core rule", "text": "No country can own the Moon, not even by landing first.", "highlight": "Claims by sovereignty, use or occupation are all barred.", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/spacelaw/activities/2020/SLC2020Presentations/SLC2020PDFPresentations/A._Newman_-_8_Dec_2020_-_Fundamentals_of_Space_Law_.pdf", "label": "UNOOSA, fundamentals of space law"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "accords", "eyebrow": "The new layer", "text": "The Artemis Accords had 76 countries by September 2026.", "highlight": "They started in 2020 with eight founding nations.", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-celebrates-as-artemis-accords-surpasses-75-signatories/", "label": "NASA, Artemis Accords pass 75 signatories"}]}, {"kind": "info", "id": "flag", "eyebrow": "Who answers", "title": "Companies fly under their country's rules", "body": "Countries answer for what their companies do in space, so private missions need national authorization and supervision.", "bullets": ["Nearly two-thirds of Outer Space Treaty countries have also signed the Artemis Accords."], "sources": [{"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/spacelaw/activities/2020/SLC2020Presentations/SLC2020PDFPresentations/A._Newman_-_8_Dec_2020_-_Fundamentals_of_Space_Law_.pdf", "label": "UNOOSA, fundamentals of space law"}, {"url": "https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-celebrates-as-artemis-accords-surpasses-75-signatories/", "label": "NASA, Artemis Accords pass 75 signatories"}]}, {"kind": "spotFake", "id": "fake", "prompt": "One of these is false. Tap it.", "statements": ["The Outer Space Treaty dates from 1967.", "A country can claim lunar land by landing there first.", "Countries answer for their companies' activities in space."], "fakeIndex": 1, "explanation": "Article II bars national appropriation by any means, including use or occupation."}, {"kind": "theCall", "id": "call", "situation": "Your startup plans to land a rover on the Moon and stake a claim to the site.", "prompt": "What is the right plan?", "options": ["Register the land as your company's property", "Fly without telling any government", "Plan to use the site, not to own it"], "consequences": ["The treaty bars appropriation, so there is no title to register.", "Your country must authorize and supervise your mission.", "Exploring and using is allowed; claiming territory is not."], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The treaty bars claims of ownership, and your government must authorize the mission."}, {"id": "lens-career", "kind": "info", "title": "Know the 1967 baseline", "body": "Space law starts with the Outer Space Treaty: no national claims, and countries answer for their companies.", "eyebrow": "For your interview", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/spacelaw/activities/2020/SLC2020Presentations/SLC2020PDFPresentations/A._Newman_-_8_Dec_2020_-_Fundamentals_of_Space_Law_.pdf", "label": "UNOOSA, fundamentals of space law"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In space, you can explore and use, but no one can claim territory.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/spacelaw/activities/2020/SLC2020Presentations/SLC2020PDFPresentations/A._Newman_-_8_Dec_2020_-_Fundamentals_of_Space_Law_.pdf", "label": "UNOOSA, fundamentals of space law"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "The 1967 Outer Space Treaty bars any country from claiming the Moon and makes countries answer for their companies. The Artemis Accords had 76 signatories by September 2026.", "sectionKey": "risk"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
