 Spectrum", "text": "The FCC licenses the radio signals a satellite uses.", "highlight": "It also sets rules like the five-year deorbit limit.", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.satellitetoday.com/government-military/2022/09/30/fcc-adopts-5-year-rule-for-deorbiting-satellites/", "label": "Via Satellite, FCC five-year deorbit rule"}]}, {"kind": "info", "id": "imaging", "eyebrow": "License 3 · Imaging", "title": "Imaging needs a third license", "body": "Private operators of remote sensing satellites are licensed by Commerce's Office of Space Commerce.", "keyTerms": [{"term": "Remote sensing", "definition": "Observing Earth from space, for example with cameras or radar."}], "sources": [{"url": "https://space.commerce.gov/regulations/commercial-remote-sensing-regulatory-affairs/", "label": "Office of Space Commerce, remote sensing licensing"}]}, {"kind": "sortSignal", "id": "sort", "prompt": "Which agency licenses it?", "buckets": ["FAA", "FCC", "Commerce"], "items": [{"text": "A rocket launch", "bucket": 0}, {"text": "A capsule's reentry", "bucket": 0}, {"text": "A satellite's radio signals", "bucket": 1}, {"text": "A private imaging satellite", "bucket": 2}], "explanation": "FAA for launch and reentry, FCC for spectrum, Commerce for private remote sensing."}, {"kind": "multipleChoice", "id": "mc", "prompt": "Why does a private space mission need government approval at all?", "options": ["Governments own every launch site in the country", "Countries answer for their companies in space", "The United Nations owns outer space"], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Under the Outer Space Treaty, countries are responsible for national activities in space, including private ones."}, {"id": "lens-curiosity", "kind": "info", "title": "Your country signs for you", "body": "Under a 1967 treaty, countries answer for their companies in space, so private missions need approval.", "eyebrow": "Why it matters", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/spacelaw/activities/2020/SLC2020Presentations/SLC2020PDFPresentations/A._Newman_-_8_Dec_2020_-_Fundamentals_of_Space_Law_.pdf", "label": "UNOOSA, fundamentals of space law"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In space, you fly on your country's permission.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/spacelaw/activities/2020/SLC2020Presentations/SLC2020PDFPresentations/A._Newman_-_8_Dec_2020_-_Fundamentals_of_Space_Law_.pdf", "label": "UNOOSA, fundamentals of space law"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "US missions can need an FAA launch license, an FCC spectrum license and, for imaging, a Commerce license. Countries answer for their companies under the 1967 treaty.", "sectionKey": "basics"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$4b513d56-b2ba-577d-bf2c-d9414a64ab09$ml$::uuid, $ml$spacetech$ml$, $ml$Who pays if a rocket hits a house$ml$, 'LESSON', ARRAY[$ml$MICRO_LESSON$ml$, $ml$day-26$ml$, $ml$month-1$ml$, $ml$handwritten-v1$ml$, $ml$goal:career$ml$, $ml$level:beginner$ml$, $ml$level:intermediate$ml$, $ml$level:advanced$ml$]::text[], 5, now(), '{}'::jsonb, $ml${"id": "spacetech-d26-career", "title": "Who pays if a rocket hits a house", "exercises": [{"id": "open", "kind": "coldOpen", "kicker": "First the insurer, then the government.", "eyebrow": "Day 26 · The money", "headline": "US launch companies must insure against damage to the public, on average about $99 million per launch.", "fullText": "The FAA works out each launch's maximum probable loss and requires insurance for that amount. Above it, the federal government may cover claims up to an extra $1.5 billion in 1988 dollars, about $2.7 billion by 2012, if Congress approves the money. That backstop has never been paid.", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.gao.gov/products/GAO-12-899", "label": "GAO, commercial space launch insurance and indemnification"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "mpl", "eyebrow": "Layer 1", "text": "Maximum probable loss is the FAA's estimate of the worst likely damage to others.", "highlight": "The launch company must insure up to that amount.", "keyTerms": [{"term": "Maximum probable loss", "definition": "The FAA estimate of likely damage to others from a launch, which sets the insurance required."}], "sources": [{"url": "https://www.gao.gov/products/GAO-12-899", "label": "GAO, commercial space launch insurance and indemnification"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "backstop", "eyebrow": "Layer 2", "text": "Above the insurance, taxpayers stand behind the launch.", "highlight": "Up to about $2.7 billion more, if Congress approves it.", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.gao.gov/products/GAO-12-899", "label": "GAO, commercial space launch insurance and indemnification"}]}, {"kind": "info", "id": "method", "eyebrow": "The weak spot", "title": "The method was showing its age", "body": "GAO found the FAA's method dated from 1988 and used a $3 million cost per casualty from 2002 data.", "bullets": ["A later review suggested $5 to 6 million per casualty."], "sources": [{"url": "https://www.gao.gov/products/GAO-12-899", "label": "GAO, commercial space launch insurance and indemnification"}]}, {"kind": "numberSense", "id": "ns-mpl", "prompt": "On average, how much third-party insurance did the FAA require per launch, in millions of dollars?", "min": 0, "max": 500, "value": 99, "unit": "M", "explanation": "About $99 million, set by each launch's maximum probable loss."}, {"kind": "faceOff", "id": "face", "prompt": "A launch damages someone's property. Who pays first?", "left": {"name": "The US government", "note": "Covers claims above the insurance"}, "right": {"name": "The launch company's insurer", "note": "Covers up to the maximum probable loss"}, "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Insurance pays first, up to the maximum probable loss. The government backstop sits above it."}, {"id": "lens-career", "kind": "info", "title": "Know the liability layers", "body": "Insurance first, then a government backstop. Explaining those layers shows you understand launch risk.", "eyebrow": "For your interview", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.gao.gov/products/GAO-12-899", "label": "GAO, commercial space launch insurance and indemnification"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In launch, the public is protected by insurance first and taxpayers second.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.gao.gov/products/GAO-12-899", "label": "GAO, commercial space launch insurance and indemnification"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "The FAA sets required third-party insurance from each launch's maximum probable loss, about $99 million on average. Above that, the government may cover up to about $2.7 billion.", "sectionKey": "how_money_moves"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$be068b4c-7d4b-59a6-b067-937b837b469a$ml$::uuid, $ml$spacetech$ml$, $ml$Who pays if a rocket hits a house$ml$, 'LESSON', ARRAY[$ml$MICRO_LESSON$ml$, $ml$day-26$ml$, $ml$month-1$ml$, $ml$handwritten-v1$ml$, $ml$goal:invest$ml$, $ml$level:beginner$ml$, $ml$level:intermediate$ml$, $ml$level:advanced$ml$]::text[], 5, now(), '{}'::jsonb, $ml${"id": "spacetech-d26-invest", "title": "Who pays if a rocket hits a house", "exercises": [{"id": "open", "kind": "coldOpen", "kicker": "First the insurer, then the government.", "eyebrow": "Day 26 · The money", "headline": "US launch companies must insure against damage to the public, on average about $99 million per launch.", "fullText": "The FAA works out each launch's maximum probable loss and requires insurance for that amount. Above it, the federal government may cover claims up to an extra $1.5 billion in 1988 dollars, about $2.7 billion by 2012, if Congress approves the money. That backstop has never been paid.", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.gao.gov/products/GAO-12-899", "label": "GAO, commercial space launch insurance and indemnification"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "mpl", "eyebrow": "Layer 1", "text": "Maximum probable loss is the FAA's estimate of the worst likely damage to others.", "highlight": "The launch company must insure up to that amount.", "keyTerms": [{"term": "Maximum probable loss", "definition": "The FAA estimate of likely damage to others from a launch, which sets the insurance required."}], "sources": [{"url": "https://www.gao.gov/products/GAO-12-899", "label": "GAO, commercial space launch insurance and indemnification"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "backstop", "eyebrow": "Layer 2", "text": "Above the insurance, taxpayers stand behind the launch.", "highlight": "Up to about $2.7 billion more, if Congress approves it.", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.gao.gov/products/GAO-12-899", "label": "GAO, commercial space launch insurance and indemnification"}]}, {"kind": "info", "id": "method", "eyebrow": "The weak spot", "title": "The method was showing its age", "body": "GAO found the FAA's method dated from 1988 and used a $3 million cost per casualty from 2002 data.", "bullets": ["A later review suggested $5 to 6 million per casualty."], "sources": [{"url": "https://www.gao.gov/products/GAO-12-899", "label": "GAO, commercial space launch insurance and indemnification"}]}, {"kind": "numberSense", "id": "ns-mpl", "prompt": "On average, how much third-party insurance did the FAA require per launch, in millions of dollars?", "min": 0, "max": 500, "value": 99, "unit": "M", "explanation": "About $99 million, set by each launch's maximum probable loss."}, {"kind": "faceOff", "id": "face", "prompt": "A launch damages someone's property. Who pays first?", "left": {"name": "The US government", "note": "Covers claims above the insurance"}, "right": {"name": "The launch company's insurer", "note": "Covers up to the maximum probable loss"}, "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Insurance pays first, up to the maximum probable loss. The government backstop sits above it."}, {"id": "lens-invest", "kind": "info", "title": "The backstop keeps insurance affordable", "body": "Government cover above the insurance limits what launch companies must buy. Changes to it would raise their costs.", "eyebrow": "For investors", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.gao.gov/products/GAO-12-899", "label": "GAO, commercial space launch insurance and indemnification"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In launch, the public is protected by insurance first and taxpayers second.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.gao.gov/products/GAO-12-899", "label": "GAO, commercial space launch insurance and indemnification"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "The FAA sets required third-party insurance from each launch's maximum probable loss, about $99 million on average. Above that, the government may cover up to about $2.7 billion.", "sectionKey": "drivers"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
