wer to say no.", "highlight": "If they answer for the damage, they decide who flies.", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/spacelaw/activities/2020/SLC2020Presentations/SLC2020PDFPresentations/A._Newman_-_8_Dec_2020_-_Fundamentals_of_Space_Law_.pdf", "label": "UNOOSA, fundamentals of space law"}]}, {"kind": "info", "id": "settlement", "eyebrow": "The outcome", "title": "Canada accepted a full settlement", "body": "The 1981 protocol settled all claims from the breakup for C$3 million.", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/oosa/en/ourwork/spacelaw/nationalspacelaw/bi-multi-lateral-agreements/can_ussr_001.html", "label": "UNOOSA, Canada-USSR Cosmos 954 settlement"}]}, {"kind": "multipleChoice", "id": "mc", "prompt": "Who was liable for the damage from Cosmos 954?", "options": ["The satellite's private builder", "The United Nations space office", "The USSR, as the launching state"], "correctIndex": 2, "explanation": "The treaty makes the launching country liable. The USSR paid Canada C$3 million."}, {"kind": "theCall", "id": "call", "situation": "A foreign startup wants to launch from your country's spaceport.", "prompt": "As the regulator, what do you require?", "options": ["Nothing, since its home country is liable", "Proof of safety and insurance first", "A ban on all foreign payloads"], "consequences": ["The country of the launch site is liable too.", "You carry the liability, so you check the risk.", "A ban gives up the business entirely."], "correctIndex": 1, "explanation": "Launches from your territory make your country liable, so you license them carefully."}, {"id": "lens-invest", "kind": "info", "title": "Liability shapes regulation", "body": "Countries carry the liability, so they set strict launch rules. Regulatory pace is part of every launch timeline.", "eyebrow": "For investors", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/spacelaw/activities/2020/SLC2020Presentations/SLC2020PDFPresentations/A._Newman_-_8_Dec_2020_-_Fundamentals_of_Space_Law_.pdf", "label": "UNOOSA, fundamentals of space law"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In space, whoever carries the liability holds the power to say no.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/spacelaw/activities/2020/SLC2020Presentations/SLC2020PDFPresentations/A._Newman_-_8_Dec_2020_-_Fundamentals_of_Space_Law_.pdf", "label": "UNOOSA, fundamentals of space law"}, {"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/oosa/en/ourwork/spacelaw/nationalspacelaw/bi-multi-lateral-agreements/can_ussr_001.html", "label": "UNOOSA, Canada-USSR Cosmos 954 settlement"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "Countries are liable for damage from what they launch, as the Soviet Union was when Cosmos 954 fell on Canada. That liability is why governments control who flies.", "sectionKey": "thesis"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
