mos 954 settlement"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In space, whoever carries the liability holds the power to say no.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/spacelaw/activities/2020/SLC2020Presentations/SLC2020PDFPresentations/A._Newman_-_8_Dec_2020_-_Fundamentals_of_Space_Law_.pdf", "label": "UNOOSA, fundamentals of space law"}, {"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/oosa/en/ourwork/spacelaw/nationalspacelaw/bi-multi-lateral-agreements/can_ussr_001.html", "label": "UNOOSA, Canada-USSR Cosmos 954 settlement"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "Countries are liable for damage from what they launch, as the Soviet Union was when Cosmos 954 fell on Canada. That liability is why governments control who flies.", "sectionKey": "players"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$8099728c-b19a-5fd7-9c51-eb8c77997516$ml$::uuid, $ml$spacetech$ml$, $ml$10%, 50%, 100%: the ITU clock$ml$, 'LESSON', ARRAY[$ml$MICRO_LESSON$ml$, $ml$day-28$ml$, $ml$month-1$ml$, $ml$handwritten-v1$ml$, $ml$goal:career$ml$, $ml$level:beginner$ml$, $ml$level:intermediate$ml$, $ml$level:advanced$ml$]::text[], 5, now(), '{}'::jsonb, $ml${"id": "spacetech-d28-career", "title": "10%, 50%, 100%: the ITU clock", "exercises": [{"id": "open", "kind": "coldOpen", "kicker": "File for spectrum, then race the clock.", "eyebrow": "Day 28 · The numbers", "headline": "Since 2019, satellite constellations must deploy 10% within 2 years, 50% within 5 and all within 7.", "fullText": "The International Telecommunication Union set these milestones for non-geostationary systems at its 2019 World Radiocommunication Conference. The clock starts after the regulatory period for bringing frequencies into use ends. The goal is to keep the master frequency register close to what is actually flying and to stop spectrum warehousing.", "sources": [{"url": "https://itu.int/en/mediacentre/backgrounders/Pages/Non-geostationary-satellite-systems.aspx", "label": "ITU, non-geostationary satellite systems"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "paper", "eyebrow": "The problem", "text": "Filing at the ITU reserves frequencies on paper.", "highlight": "Milestones push operators to actually launch.", "keyTerms": [{"term": "ITU", "definition": "The UN agency that coordinates radio spectrum and satellite orbits worldwide."}], "sources": [{"url": "https://itu.int/en/mediacentre/backgrounders/Pages/Non-geostationary-satellite-systems.aspx", "label": "ITU, non-geostationary satellite systems"}]}, {"kind": "microInsight", "id": "warehousing", "eyebrow": "The target", "text": "Warehousing means holding spectrum you are not using.", "highlight": "The milestones were written to prevent it.", "keyTerms": [{"term": "Warehousing", "definition": "Reserving spectrum without using it, which keeps others out."}], "sources": [{"url": "https://itu.int/en/mediacentre/backgrounders/Pages/Non-geostationary-satellite-systems.aspx", "label": "ITU, non-geostationary satellite systems"}]}, {"kind": "info", "id": "register", "eyebrow": "The goal", "title": "The register should match the sky", "body": "The ITU wants its Master International Frequency Register to reflect what is really deployed.", "sources": [{"url": "https://itu.int/en/mediacentre/backgrounders/Pages/Non-geostationary-satellite-systems.aspx", "label": "ITU, non-geostationary satellite systems"}]}, {"kind": "numberSense", "id": "ns-first", "prompt": "A constellation filed for 3,000 satellites. Under ITU milestones, how many must be up within 2 years?", "min": 0, "max": 1000, "value": 300, "unit": "satellites", "explanation": "10% within two years: 300 of 3,000. Half, 1,500, would be due within five."}, {"kind": "spotFake", "id": "fake", "prompt": "One of these is false. Tap it.", "statements": ["Constellations must deploy 10% within two years.", "Operators get 20 years to finish deploying.", "Half must be up within five years."], "fakeIndex": 1, "explanation": "The full constellation is due within seven years."}, {"id": "lens-career", "kind": "info", "title": "Quote the milestones", "body": "10% in 2 years, 50% in 5, 100% in 7. Knowing the ITU clock shows you understand constellation planning.", "eyebrow": "For your interview", "sources": [{"url": "https://itu.int/en/mediacentre/backgrounders/Pages/Non-geostationary-satellite-systems.aspx", "label": "ITU, non-geostationary satellite systems"}]}, {"id": "rule", "kind": "microInsight", "text": "In space, spectrum is reserved on paper but kept by launching.", "eyebrow": "The rule", "sources": [{"url": "https://itu.int/en/mediacentre/backgrounders/Pages/Non-geostationary-satellite-systems.aspx", "label": "ITU, non-geostationary satellite systems"}]}, {"id": "say-it", "kind": "sayIt", "takeaway": "ITU milestones require constellations to deploy 10% within 2 years, 50% within 5 and 100% within 7. They keep the frequency register close to what is actually flying.", "sectionKey": "numbers"}]}$ml$::jsonb),
