($ml$c3601775-3645-5229-925c-0347254bdad2$ml$::uuid, $ml$8d141a41-0ab3-5706-b424-aef32d6a85f7$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 14 · The money$ml$, $ml$In 2023, space insurers paid $995 million in claims but collected only about $557 million. Two satellites caused most of the damage: ViaSat-3 Americas led to a $445 million claim and Inmarsat-6 F2, after a battery failure, to $348 million. The net loss of $438 million was a record. The premium for a typical geostationary satellite on a Falcon 9 rose from under 6% of its insured value to about 10%.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.satellitetoday.com/sustainability/2024/05/01/slingshot-aerospace-reveals-record-insurance-losses-in-2023-in-new-satellite-deployments-report", "label": "Via Satellite, record space insurance losses in 2023"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$cbf2ece0-be4f-5753-93b6-f5d23d69c1e0$ml$::uuid, $ml$8d141a41-0ab3-5706-b424-aef32d6a85f7$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$How it works$ml$, $ml$Operators pay a premium: a share of the satellite's insured value. For a geostationary satellite on Falcon 9, it rose from under 6% to about 10% in 2023.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.satellitetoday.com/sustainability/2024/05/01/slingshot-aerospace-reveals-record-insurance-losses-in-2023-in-new-satellite-deployments-report", "label": "Via Satellite, record space insurance losses in 2023"}]$ml$::jsonb),
