($ml$5fdac135-fb4b-5501-8a59-8092db4d76b7$ml$::uuid, $ml$e9dc72c2-bc6a-545c-b7fd-66022bbf6f23$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In European space, where the money comes from decides where the work goes.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.esa.int/About_Us/Business_with_ESA/Business_Opportunities/ESA_an_intergovernmental_customer", "label": "ESA, an intergovernmental customer"}, {"url": "https://europeanspaceflight.com/esa-director-general-reaffirms-geo-return-policy-as-fundamental/", "label": "European Spaceflight, geo-return reaffirmed"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$867d12cc-36ac-5b92-9a12-6224a439279f$ml$::uuid, $ml$897472e1-fa67-5270-b885-0f1c750c50be$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 21 · The power$ml$, $ml$In May 2023, NASA gave Blue Origin $3.4 billion to build a second crewed Moon lander. SpaceX had won the first lander contract in 2021 with a $2.9 billion bid for Starship. NASA then paid for a second design so it would have redundancy and competition for long-term Moon missions. Blue Origin's first crewed landing was planned for Artemis V.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://spacepolicyonline.com/?p=62197", "label": "SpacePolicyOnline, Blue Origin wins NASA's second lander"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$b0f340e3-364d-501a-9ca6-6028a33a7875$ml$::uuid, $ml$897472e1-fa67-5270-b885-0f1c750c50be$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$The logic$ml$, $ml$With one supplier, the supplier holds the cards. With two, NASA can push both on price and schedule.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://spacepolicyonline.com/?p=62197", "label": "SpacePolicyOnline, Blue Origin wins NASA's second lander"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$dbcf33d7-85f3-5535-809f-97d55410f013$ml$::uuid, $ml$897472e1-fa67-5270-b885-0f1c750c50be$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$Who pays$ml$, $ml$Blue Origin is putting in money of its own too. The total effort is estimated at about $7 billion.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://spacepolicyonline.com/?p=62197", "label": "SpacePolicyOnline, Blue Origin wins NASA's second lander"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$f3cdd025-7098-5faa-9294-3e230b50895c$ml$::uuid, $ml$897472e1-fa67-5270-b885-0f1c750c50be$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$Redundancy has a price$ml$, $ml$Paying for two landers costs more up front. NASA judged it worth it to avoid depending on one company.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://spacepolicyonline.com/?p=62197", "label": "SpacePolicyOnline, Blue Origin wins NASA's second lander"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$d4b7255a-2412-55d5-a1a7-a3d7869b9e2a$ml$::uuid, $ml$897472e1-fa67-5270-b885-0f1c750c50be$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Know why NASA buys twice$ml$, $ml$NASA paid for two landers to keep redundancy and competition. Explaining that shows procurement sense.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://spacepolicyonline.com/?p=62197", "label": "SpacePolicyOnline, Blue Origin wins NASA's second lander"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$94f11010-27ea-57a5-8313-5695369f0bea$ml$::uuid, $ml$897472e1-fa67-5270-b885-0f1c750c50be$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In space exploration, paying for a second supplier buys the buyer bargaining power.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://spacepolicyonline.com/?p=62197", "label": "SpacePolicyOnline, Blue Origin wins NASA's second lander"}]$ml$::jsonb),
