($ml$59ddc829-36ba-5c1d-b169-d70203c693a5$ml$::uuid, $ml$2240bfda-4cd5-5dcf-9958-35de83ab96a8$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 22 · The numbers$ml$, $ml$In 1966, NASA got 4.41% of the federal budget. By 2019, it got 0.49%. At the height of Apollo, about one federal dollar in 23 went to NASA. In fiscal 2019 its $21.5 billion budget was less than half a cent of each federal dollar. Congress passed $24.4 billion for fiscal 2026.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.statista.com/chart/amp/17504/nasas-budget-share-of-the-us-federal-budget", "label": "Statista, NASA's share of the federal budget"}, {"url": "https://space.com/space-exploration/in-rejection-of-trumps-deep-cuts-congress-passes-usd24-4-billion-budget-for-nasa", "label": "Space.com, NASA fiscal 2026 budget"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$590406e2-6023-5ebb-a58b-11c3b5d085f7$ml$::uuid, $ml$2240bfda-4cd5-5dcf-9958-35de83ab96a8$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$The metric$ml$, $ml$Budget share shows priority better than dollar totals. NASA's share of federal spending shrank about ninefold from 1966 to 2019.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.statista.com/chart/amp/17504/nasas-budget-share-of-the-us-federal-budget", "label": "Statista, NASA's share of the federal budget"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$947c8169-9aad-5f22-b642-3abb19cfb77d$ml$::uuid, $ml$2240bfda-4cd5-5dcf-9958-35de83ab96a8$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$The peak$ml$, $ml$The peak came with one national goal: landing on the Moon. The share topped out in 1966, three years before Apollo 11.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.statista.com/chart/amp/17504/nasas-budget-share-of-the-us-federal-budget", "label": "Statista, NASA's share of the federal budget"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$ede43133-de6c-5889-a395-cc7ab4ddbffe$ml$::uuid, $ml$2240bfda-4cd5-5dcf-9958-35de83ab96a8$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$Read budgets as shares$ml$, $ml$A rising dollar total can still be a falling priority. Compare a budget with total federal spending.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.statista.com/chart/amp/17504/nasas-budget-share-of-the-us-federal-budget", "label": "Statista, NASA's share of the federal budget"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$21e0c61e-2b6b-58a9-8ff7-00e7ce4285e3$ml$::uuid, $ml$2240bfda-4cd5-5dcf-9958-35de83ab96a8$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Half a cent per dollar$ml$, $ml$Of each federal dollar, NASA gets about half a cent, against more than four cents in 1966.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.statista.com/chart/amp/17504/nasas-budget-share-of-the-us-federal-budget", "label": "Statista, NASA's share of the federal budget"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$ff97450c-6778-534e-935d-984101909845$ml$::uuid, $ml$2240bfda-4cd5-5dcf-9958-35de83ab96a8$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In government space, a budget's share of spending shows its real priority.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.statista.com/chart/amp/17504/nasas-budget-share-of-the-us-federal-budget", "label": "Statista, NASA's share of the federal budget"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$450121b4-94e7-59ee-b3a9-cafc66631316$ml$::uuid, $ml$06a86127-eb9a-558e-bf99-6eb39b66e9bd$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 23 · The rule$ml$, $ml$Under the 1967 Outer Space Treaty, no country can claim the Moon or any part of space. Article II says outer space, the Moon and other celestial bodies are not subject to national appropriation, whether by claim of sovereignty, use, occupation or any other means. Article VI makes each country responsible for its companies' activities in space. The US-led Artemis Accords, launched in 2020 by eight nations, add practical principles on top.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/spacelaw/activities/2020/SLC2020Presentations/SLC2020PDFPresentations/A._Newman_-_8_Dec_2020_-_Fundamentals_of_Space_Law_.pdf", "label": "UNOOSA, fundamentals of space law"}, {"url": "https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-celebrates-as-artemis-accords-surpasses-75-signatories/", "label": "NASA, Artemis Accords pass 75 signatories"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$f1e7544c-3ceb-55be-a151-22a77ca0712e$ml$::uuid, $ml$06a86127-eb9a-558e-bf99-6eb39b66e9bd$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$The core rule$ml$, $ml$No country can own the Moon, not even by landing first. Claims by sovereignty, use or occupation are all barred.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/spacelaw/activities/2020/SLC2020Presentations/SLC2020PDFPresentations/A._Newman_-_8_Dec_2020_-_Fundamentals_of_Space_Law_.pdf", "label": "UNOOSA, fundamentals of space law"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$7b9b23a1-4fbb-5eab-95ba-41fb1b231e6d$ml$::uuid, $ml$06a86127-eb9a-558e-bf99-6eb39b66e9bd$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$The new layer$ml$, $ml$The Artemis Accords had 76 countries by September 2026. They started in 2020 with eight founding nations.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-celebrates-as-artemis-accords-surpasses-75-signatories/", "label": "NASA, Artemis Accords pass 75 signatories"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$1d2e1415-104b-57a2-b2f0-291ef9354759$ml$::uuid, $ml$06a86127-eb9a-558e-bf99-6eb39b66e9bd$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$Companies fly under their country's rules$ml$, $ml$Countries answer for what their companies do in space, so private missions need national authorization and supervision. Nearly two-thirds of Outer Space Treaty countries have also signed the Artemis Accords.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/spacelaw/activities/2020/SLC2020Presentations/SLC2020PDFPresentations/A._Newman_-_8_Dec_2020_-_Fundamentals_of_Space_Law_.pdf", "label": "UNOOSA, fundamentals of space law"}, {"url": "https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-celebrates-as-artemis-accords-surpasses-75-signatories/", "label": "NASA, Artemis Accords pass 75 signatories"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$331a2dcb-7ea9-51d3-98ef-31b8d73ccc59$ml$::uuid, $ml$06a86127-eb9a-558e-bf99-6eb39b66e9bd$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Know the 1967 baseline$ml$, $ml$Space law starts with the Outer Space Treaty: no national claims, and countries answer for their companies.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/spacelaw/activities/2020/SLC2020Presentations/SLC2020PDFPresentations/A._Newman_-_8_Dec_2020_-_Fundamentals_of_Space_Law_.pdf", "label": "UNOOSA, fundamentals of space law"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$1f1e42a6-4d5d-5898-900a-75ab3a8eee17$ml$::uuid, $ml$06a86127-eb9a-558e-bf99-6eb39b66e9bd$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In space, you can explore and use, but no one can claim territory.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/spacelaw/activities/2020/SLC2020Presentations/SLC2020PDFPresentations/A._Newman_-_8_Dec_2020_-_Fundamentals_of_Space_Law_.pdf", "label": "UNOOSA, fundamentals of space law"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$d10236ca-53e2-5f97-8f54-a4aed7c647a3$ml$::uuid, $ml$122b907a-e1de-5c69-9729-6701d03fd166$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 23 · The rule$ml$, $ml$Under the 1967 Outer Space Treaty, no country can claim the Moon or any part of space. Article II says outer space, the Moon and other celestial bodies are not subject to national appropriation, whether by claim of sovereignty, use, occupation or any other means. Article VI makes each country responsible for its companies' activities in space. The US-led Artemis Accords, launched in 2020 by eight nations, add practical principles on top.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/spacelaw/activities/2020/SLC2020Presentations/SLC2020PDFPresentations/A._Newman_-_8_Dec_2020_-_Fundamentals_of_Space_Law_.pdf", "label": "UNOOSA, fundamentals of space law"}, {"url": "https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-celebrates-as-artemis-accords-surpasses-75-signatories/", "label": "NASA, Artemis Accords pass 75 signatories"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$fc0ee840-4aad-5dc8-a58f-1bdf2cbba526$ml$::uuid, $ml$122b907a-e1de-5c69-9729-6701d03fd166$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$The core rule$ml$, $ml$No country can own the Moon, not even by landing first. Claims by sovereignty, use or occupation are all barred.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/spacelaw/activities/2020/SLC2020Presentations/SLC2020PDFPresentations/A._Newman_-_8_Dec_2020_-_Fundamentals_of_Space_Law_.pdf", "label": "UNOOSA, fundamentals of space law"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$24c657cc-9e71-56d4-8eab-37dab3223d53$ml$::uuid, $ml$122b907a-e1de-5c69-9729-6701d03fd166$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$The new layer$ml$, $ml$The Artemis Accords had 76 countries by September 2026. They started in 2020 with eight founding nations.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-celebrates-as-artemis-accords-surpasses-75-signatories/", "label": "NASA, Artemis Accords pass 75 signatories"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$c961e7c5-4bf2-5a15-9f11-72ec0eea61cd$ml$::uuid, $ml$122b907a-e1de-5c69-9729-6701d03fd166$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$Companies fly under their country's rules$ml$, $ml$Countries answer for what their companies do in space, so private missions need national authorization and supervision. Nearly two-thirds of Outer Space Treaty countries have also signed the Artemis Accords.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/spacelaw/activities/2020/SLC2020Presentations/SLC2020PDFPresentations/A._Newman_-_8_Dec_2020_-_Fundamentals_of_Space_Law_.pdf", "label": "UNOOSA, fundamentals of space law"}, {"url": "https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-celebrates-as-artemis-accords-surpasses-75-signatories/", "label": "NASA, Artemis Accords pass 75 signatories"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$4da2164c-71b8-5156-9fbc-6c248e1702b0$ml$::uuid, $ml$122b907a-e1de-5c69-9729-6701d03fd166$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Rights shape returns$ml$, $ml$No one can own lunar land. Business cases must rest on operations and services, not territory.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/spacelaw/activities/2020/SLC2020Presentations/SLC2020PDFPresentations/A._Newman_-_8_Dec_2020_-_Fundamentals_of_Space_Law_.pdf", "label": "UNOOSA, fundamentals of space law"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$481aa7cb-082e-5a3f-a05d-aeb315f85029$ml$::uuid, $ml$122b907a-e1de-5c69-9729-6701d03fd166$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In space, you can explore and use, but no one can claim territory.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/spacelaw/activities/2020/SLC2020Presentations/SLC2020PDFPresentations/A._Newman_-_8_Dec_2020_-_Fundamentals_of_Space_Law_.pdf", "label": "UNOOSA, fundamentals of space law"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$09a2d3de-9b5a-593b-9b7a-37d2889b0175$ml$::uuid, $ml$41059983-e4f9-58c3-9049-b2ce381bc121$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 23 · The rule$ml$, $ml$Under the 1967 Outer Space Treaty, no country can claim the Moon or any part of space. Article II says outer space, the Moon and other celestial bodies are not subject to national appropriation, whether by claim of sovereignty, use, occupation or any other means. Article VI makes each country responsible for its companies' activities in space. The US-led Artemis Accords, launched in 2020 by eight nations, add practical principles on top.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/spacelaw/activities/2020/SLC2020Presentations/SLC2020PDFPresentations/A._Newman_-_8_Dec_2020_-_Fundamentals_of_Space_Law_.pdf", "label": "UNOOSA, fundamentals of space law"}, {"url": "https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-celebrates-as-artemis-accords-surpasses-75-signatories/", "label": "NASA, Artemis Accords pass 75 signatories"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$2ef44c24-a83a-5909-9e32-49c6c82fb968$ml$::uuid, $ml$41059983-e4f9-58c3-9049-b2ce381bc121$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$The core rule$ml$, $ml$No country can own the Moon, not even by landing first. Claims by sovereignty, use or occupation are all barred.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/spacelaw/activities/2020/SLC2020Presentations/SLC2020PDFPresentations/A._Newman_-_8_Dec_2020_-_Fundamentals_of_Space_Law_.pdf", "label": "UNOOSA, fundamentals of space law"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$bcdbbc21-9557-5d64-9091-52a2f089783b$ml$::uuid, $ml$41059983-e4f9-58c3-9049-b2ce381bc121$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$The new layer$ml$, $ml$The Artemis Accords had 76 countries by September 2026. They started in 2020 with eight founding nations.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-celebrates-as-artemis-accords-surpasses-75-signatories/", "label": "NASA, Artemis Accords pass 75 signatories"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$6848cd43-bfa4-5107-8fad-1547c16ad4ef$ml$::uuid, $ml$41059983-e4f9-58c3-9049-b2ce381bc121$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$Companies fly under their country's rules$ml$, $ml$Countries answer for what their companies do in space, so private missions need national authorization and supervision. Nearly two-thirds of Outer Space Treaty countries have also signed the Artemis Accords.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/spacelaw/activities/2020/SLC2020Presentations/SLC2020PDFPresentations/A._Newman_-_8_Dec_2020_-_Fundamentals_of_Space_Law_.pdf", "label": "UNOOSA, fundamentals of space law"}, {"url": "https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-celebrates-as-artemis-accords-surpasses-75-signatories/", "label": "NASA, Artemis Accords pass 75 signatories"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$0ac8895b-aaaa-503a-b216-d5367673d957$ml$::uuid, $ml$41059983-e4f9-58c3-9049-b2ce381bc121$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Use it, don't own it$ml$, $ml$Plans that depend on owning Moon land will not fly. Plans built on using sites and selling services can.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/spacelaw/activities/2020/SLC2020Presentations/SLC2020PDFPresentations/A._Newman_-_8_Dec_2020_-_Fundamentals_of_Space_Law_.pdf", "label": "UNOOSA, fundamentals of space law"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$13cee640-bc4d-5ccc-8a4e-26b614d04534$ml$::uuid, $ml$41059983-e4f9-58c3-9049-b2ce381bc121$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In space, you can explore and use, but no one can claim territory.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/spacelaw/activities/2020/SLC2020Presentations/SLC2020PDFPresentations/A._Newman_-_8_Dec_2020_-_Fundamentals_of_Space_Law_.pdf", "label": "UNOOSA, fundamentals of space law"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$c645e05c-d0ec-5d53-9fd8-1359f9c21dbe$ml$::uuid, $ml$f38731b1-bfaa-56c1-aa2e-a805374789ef$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 23 · The rule$ml$, $ml$Under the 1967 Outer Space Treaty, no country can claim the Moon or any part of space. Article II says outer space, the Moon and other celestial bodies are not subject to national appropriation, whether by claim of sovereignty, use, occupation or any other means. Article VI makes each country responsible for its companies' activities in space. The US-led Artemis Accords, launched in 2020 by eight nations, add practical principles on top.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/spacelaw/activities/2020/SLC2020Presentations/SLC2020PDFPresentations/A._Newman_-_8_Dec_2020_-_Fundamentals_of_Space_Law_.pdf", "label": "UNOOSA, fundamentals of space law"}, {"url": "https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-celebrates-as-artemis-accords-surpasses-75-signatories/", "label": "NASA, Artemis Accords pass 75 signatories"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$51452023-0e2e-5abd-ac19-4f36819a3755$ml$::uuid, $ml$f38731b1-bfaa-56c1-aa2e-a805374789ef$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$The core rule$ml$, $ml$No country can own the Moon, not even by landing first. Claims by sovereignty, use or occupation are all barred.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/spacelaw/activities/2020/SLC2020Presentations/SLC2020PDFPresentations/A._Newman_-_8_Dec_2020_-_Fundamentals_of_Space_Law_.pdf", "label": "UNOOSA, fundamentals of space law"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$cc3199ed-1f16-515a-9820-fc6d295de2bf$ml$::uuid, $ml$f38731b1-bfaa-56c1-aa2e-a805374789ef$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$The new layer$ml$, $ml$The Artemis Accords had 76 countries by September 2026. They started in 2020 with eight founding nations.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-celebrates-as-artemis-accords-surpasses-75-signatories/", "label": "NASA, Artemis Accords pass 75 signatories"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$8849ee97-d824-5139-a468-430f433d1d12$ml$::uuid, $ml$f38731b1-bfaa-56c1-aa2e-a805374789ef$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$Companies fly under their country's rules$ml$, $ml$Countries answer for what their companies do in space, so private missions need national authorization and supervision. Nearly two-thirds of Outer Space Treaty countries have also signed the Artemis Accords.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/spacelaw/activities/2020/SLC2020Presentations/SLC2020PDFPresentations/A._Newman_-_8_Dec_2020_-_Fundamentals_of_Space_Law_.pdf", "label": "UNOOSA, fundamentals of space law"}, {"url": "https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-celebrates-as-artemis-accords-surpasses-75-signatories/", "label": "NASA, Artemis Accords pass 75 signatories"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$a4c4b04f-88a9-5583-a18a-fe517a731e95$ml$::uuid, $ml$f38731b1-bfaa-56c1-aa2e-a805374789ef$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Nobody owns the Moon$ml$, $ml$Since 1967, no country can claim the Moon, and 76 countries have signed the newer Artemis Accords.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/spacelaw/activities/2020/SLC2020Presentations/SLC2020PDFPresentations/A._Newman_-_8_Dec_2020_-_Fundamentals_of_Space_Law_.pdf", "label": "UNOOSA, fundamentals of space law"}, {"url": "https://www.nasa.gov/news-release/nasa-celebrates-as-artemis-accords-surpasses-75-signatories/", "label": "NASA, Artemis Accords pass 75 signatories"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$9f1e9aad-1867-5d93-a073-87fd9dfc2952$ml$::uuid, $ml$f38731b1-bfaa-56c1-aa2e-a805374789ef$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In space, you can explore and use, but no one can claim territory.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/spacelaw/activities/2020/SLC2020Presentations/SLC2020PDFPresentations/A._Newman_-_8_Dec_2020_-_Fundamentals_of_Space_Law_.pdf", "label": "UNOOSA, fundamentals of space law"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$a8129364-efac-52e0-9eab-dd4adda51ef7$ml$::uuid, $ml$6e62932c-ec6e-5d15-9bf9-46c8594b91d3$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 24 · Where it breaks$ml$, $ml$Perseverance has collected more than 30 Mars samples, and NASA has no funded way to bring them back. An independent review put Mars Sample Return at up to $11 billion, with samples not home until 2040. NASA asked industry for a cheaper, faster plan in 2024. In January 2026, Congress passed a NASA budget that does not support the existing program. China plans to launch Tianwen-3 in 2028 and return samples in 2031.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.livescience.com/space/mars/nasas-mars-sample-return-is-dead-leaving-china-to-retrieve-signs-of-life-from-the-red-planet", "label": "Live Science, Mars Sample Return cancelled"}, {"url": "https://spaceflightnow.com/2024/04/16/nasa-requests-proposals-to-reduce-cost-timeline-of-mars-sample-return-mission/", "label": "Spaceflight Now, NASA seeks cheaper Mars Sample Return"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$1a467967-eb6b-5b30-97f3-9a7cd552ac67$ml$::uuid, $ml$6e62932c-ec6e-5d15-9bf9-46c8594b91d3$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$The cost$ml$, $ml$Estimates ballooned from $5 to $7 billion to as much as $11 billion. The review projected $850 million to $1 billion a year during development.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://spaceflightnow.com/2024/04/16/nasa-requests-proposals-to-reduce-cost-timeline-of-mars-sample-return-mission/", "label": "Spaceflight Now, NASA seeks cheaper Mars Sample Return"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$a12ae685-fb10-50d5-9080-738fdac9530a$ml$::uuid, $ml$6e62932c-ec6e-5d15-9bf9-46c8594b91d3$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$The reset$ml$, $ml$NASA called the old plan too costly and too slow. In April 2024 it asked industry and its own centers for a quicker, cheaper design.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://spaceflightnow.com/2024/04/16/nasa-requests-proposals-to-reduce-cost-timeline-of-mars-sample-return-mission/", "label": "Spaceflight Now, NASA seeks cheaper Mars Sample Return"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$8e1a4827-2a1d-5f0a-abe3-ac74b64360b6$ml$::uuid, $ml$6e62932c-ec6e-5d15-9bf9-46c8594b91d3$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$China may get there first$ml$, $ml$Tianwen-3 aims to launch in 2028 and return rocks in 2031, from an easier but less promising site. Congress kept $110 million for future Mars mission work.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.livescience.com/space/mars/nasas-mars-sample-return-is-dead-leaving-china-to-retrieve-signs-of-life-from-the-red-planet", "label": "Live Science, Mars Sample Return cancelled"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$d68b7dab-f33f-53eb-846c-39932f44873d$ml$::uuid, $ml$6e62932c-ec6e-5d15-9bf9-46c8594b91d3$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Know why big missions stall$ml$, $ml$Mars Sample Return shows how cost growth can stop a flagship. Be ready to talk about cost and schedule discipline.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://spaceflightnow.com/2024/04/16/nasa-requests-proposals-to-reduce-cost-timeline-of-mars-sample-return-mission/", "label": "Spaceflight Now, NASA seeks cheaper Mars Sample Return"}, {"url": "https://www.livescience.com/space/mars/nasas-mars-sample-return-is-dead-leaving-china-to-retrieve-signs-of-life-from-the-red-planet", "label": "Live Science, Mars Sample Return cancelled"}]$ml$::jsonb),
