($ml$54e57162-ce15-580f-b9ca-2c95582d82e5$ml$::uuid, $ml$991febed-9689-5b63-8c9a-92d3935db565$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$License 1 · Launch$ml$, $ml$The FAA licenses launches and reentries. Its job is protecting the public on the ground and in the air.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.faa.gov/space", "label": "FAA, commercial space transportation"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$13e1ab44-f88a-5339-9275-fa928fb5733b$ml$::uuid, $ml$991febed-9689-5b63-8c9a-92d3935db565$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$License 2 · Spectrum$ml$, $ml$The FCC licenses the radio signals a satellite uses. It also sets rules like the five-year deorbit limit.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.satellitetoday.com/government-military/2022/09/30/fcc-adopts-5-year-rule-for-deorbiting-satellites/", "label": "Via Satellite, FCC five-year deorbit rule"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$c7337e00-3be4-54d2-a669-b674e4af1167$ml$::uuid, $ml$991febed-9689-5b63-8c9a-92d3935db565$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$Imaging needs a third license$ml$, $ml$Private operators of remote sensing satellites are licensed by Commerce's Office of Space Commerce.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://space.commerce.gov/regulations/commercial-remote-sensing-regulatory-affairs/", "label": "Office of Space Commerce, remote sensing licensing"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$1e9cb1bb-c9a8-5011-9dbf-7aee90162872$ml$::uuid, $ml$991febed-9689-5b63-8c9a-92d3935db565$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Budget for approvals$ml$, $ml$Several agencies may need to sign off before your satellite works. Start the applications early.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.faa.gov/space", "label": "FAA, commercial space transportation"}, {"url": "https://www.satellitetoday.com/government-military/2022/09/30/fcc-adopts-5-year-rule-for-deorbiting-satellites/", "label": "Via Satellite, FCC five-year deorbit rule"}, {"url": "https://space.commerce.gov/regulations/commercial-remote-sensing-regulatory-affairs/", "label": "Office of Space Commerce, remote sensing licensing"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$9b24a028-82fb-5720-be91-a0ff9e4f5706$ml$::uuid, $ml$991febed-9689-5b63-8c9a-92d3935db565$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In space, you fly on your country's permission.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/spacelaw/activities/2020/SLC2020Presentations/SLC2020PDFPresentations/A._Newman_-_8_Dec_2020_-_Fundamentals_of_Space_Law_.pdf", "label": "UNOOSA, fundamentals of space law"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$b3a2129d-89c1-5cd8-806b-d934da21afa8$ml$::uuid, $ml$200e0c37-8c98-5bdc-a69c-5eba928fe370$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 25 · Licensing$ml$, $ml$Getting a US imaging satellite to work involves three federal licenses. The FAA licenses the launch, the FCC licenses the satellite's radio signals, and the Commerce Department licenses private remote sensing systems. The reason goes back to the 1967 Outer Space Treaty: countries answer for their companies in space, so private missions need authorization and continuing supervision.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.faa.gov/space", "label": "FAA, commercial space transportation"}, {"url": "https://www.satellitetoday.com/government-military/2022/09/30/fcc-adopts-5-year-rule-for-deorbiting-satellites/", "label": "Via Satellite, FCC five-year deorbit rule"}, {"url": "https://space.commerce.gov/regulations/commercial-remote-sensing-regulatory-affairs/", "label": "Office of Space Commerce, remote sensing licensing"}, {"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/spacelaw/activities/2020/SLC2020Presentations/SLC2020PDFPresentations/A._Newman_-_8_Dec_2020_-_Fundamentals_of_Space_Law_.pdf", "label": "UNOOSA, fundamentals of space law"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$75db9629-9e5f-577e-a477-9b7a27d869c0$ml$::uuid, $ml$200e0c37-8c98-5bdc-a69c-5eba928fe370$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$License 1 · Launch$ml$, $ml$The FAA licenses launches and reentries. Its job is protecting the public on the ground and in the air.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.faa.gov/space", "label": "FAA, commercial space transportation"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$3316d609-d6b3-593f-a2b5-cd16110c085a$ml$::uuid, $ml$200e0c37-8c98-5bdc-a69c-5eba928fe370$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$License 2 · Spectrum$ml$, $ml$The FCC licenses the radio signals a satellite uses. It also sets rules like the five-year deorbit limit.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.satellitetoday.com/government-military/2022/09/30/fcc-adopts-5-year-rule-for-deorbiting-satellites/", "label": "Via Satellite, FCC five-year deorbit rule"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$600d5ec3-1053-5d9e-8422-92c0e4d31343$ml$::uuid, $ml$200e0c37-8c98-5bdc-a69c-5eba928fe370$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$Imaging needs a third license$ml$, $ml$Private operators of remote sensing satellites are licensed by Commerce's Office of Space Commerce.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://space.commerce.gov/regulations/commercial-remote-sensing-regulatory-affairs/", "label": "Office of Space Commerce, remote sensing licensing"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$7c914813-5ed0-584d-a92c-f957676df3b8$ml$::uuid, $ml$200e0c37-8c98-5bdc-a69c-5eba928fe370$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Your country signs for you$ml$, $ml$Under a 1967 treaty, countries answer for their companies in space, so private missions need approval.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/spacelaw/activities/2020/SLC2020Presentations/SLC2020PDFPresentations/A._Newman_-_8_Dec_2020_-_Fundamentals_of_Space_Law_.pdf", "label": "UNOOSA, fundamentals of space law"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$027a6236-d921-51ca-9f42-4ed4cc63c488$ml$::uuid, $ml$200e0c37-8c98-5bdc-a69c-5eba928fe370$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In space, you fly on your country's permission.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/spacelaw/activities/2020/SLC2020Presentations/SLC2020PDFPresentations/A._Newman_-_8_Dec_2020_-_Fundamentals_of_Space_Law_.pdf", "label": "UNOOSA, fundamentals of space law"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$6e8e8a0e-dd86-515b-a50e-ab11a57b29ca$ml$::uuid, $ml$4b513d56-b2ba-577d-bf2c-d9414a64ab09$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 26 · The money$ml$, $ml$US launch companies must insure against damage to the public, on average about $99 million per launch. The FAA works out each launch's maximum probable loss and requires insurance for that amount. Above it, the federal government may cover claims up to an extra $1.5 billion in 1988 dollars, about $2.7 billion by 2012, if Congress approves the money. That backstop has never been paid.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.gao.gov/products/GAO-12-899", "label": "GAO, commercial space launch insurance and indemnification"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$b1fb73f9-f3d2-5270-9e0b-a2b82fe4bd29$ml$::uuid, $ml$4b513d56-b2ba-577d-bf2c-d9414a64ab09$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$Layer 1$ml$, $ml$Maximum probable loss is the FAA's estimate of the worst likely damage to others. The launch company must insure up to that amount.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.gao.gov/products/GAO-12-899", "label": "GAO, commercial space launch insurance and indemnification"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$74923ca7-3447-5c88-959d-9bcd13ba3d0f$ml$::uuid, $ml$4b513d56-b2ba-577d-bf2c-d9414a64ab09$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$Layer 2$ml$, $ml$Above the insurance, taxpayers stand behind the launch. Up to about $2.7 billion more, if Congress approves it.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.gao.gov/products/GAO-12-899", "label": "GAO, commercial space launch insurance and indemnification"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$3660b0b4-fe18-518d-b897-6349ab231470$ml$::uuid, $ml$4b513d56-b2ba-577d-bf2c-d9414a64ab09$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$The method was showing its age$ml$, $ml$GAO found the FAA's method dated from 1988 and used a $3 million cost per casualty from 2002 data. A later review suggested $5 to 6 million per casualty.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.gao.gov/products/GAO-12-899", "label": "GAO, commercial space launch insurance and indemnification"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$b8b8fc1f-d449-54cb-824f-5df4890de17a$ml$::uuid, $ml$4b513d56-b2ba-577d-bf2c-d9414a64ab09$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Know the liability layers$ml$, $ml$Insurance first, then a government backstop. Explaining those layers shows you understand launch risk.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.gao.gov/products/GAO-12-899", "label": "GAO, commercial space launch insurance and indemnification"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$5388033d-a404-51f8-8be8-730aaf4d1773$ml$::uuid, $ml$4b513d56-b2ba-577d-bf2c-d9414a64ab09$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In launch, the public is protected by insurance first and taxpayers second.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.gao.gov/products/GAO-12-899", "label": "GAO, commercial space launch insurance and indemnification"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$a7f676ed-b925-5f4a-92c9-8bc3c81d5931$ml$::uuid, $ml$be068b4c-7d4b-59a6-b067-937b837b469a$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 26 · The money$ml$, $ml$US launch companies must insure against damage to the public, on average about $99 million per launch. The FAA works out each launch's maximum probable loss and requires insurance for that amount. Above it, the federal government may cover claims up to an extra $1.5 billion in 1988 dollars, about $2.7 billion by 2012, if Congress approves the money. That backstop has never been paid.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.gao.gov/products/GAO-12-899", "label": "GAO, commercial space launch insurance and indemnification"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$f98dc4cc-b1fa-5872-8035-fd14c35d4eb9$ml$::uuid, $ml$be068b4c-7d4b-59a6-b067-937b837b469a$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$Layer 1$ml$, $ml$Maximum probable loss is the FAA's estimate of the worst likely damage to others. The launch company must insure up to that amount.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.gao.gov/products/GAO-12-899", "label": "GAO, commercial space launch insurance and indemnification"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$550c6ec3-c2ce-5d79-be31-f714b3f09de0$ml$::uuid, $ml$be068b4c-7d4b-59a6-b067-937b837b469a$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$Layer 2$ml$, $ml$Above the insurance, taxpayers stand behind the launch. Up to about $2.7 billion more, if Congress approves it.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.gao.gov/products/GAO-12-899", "label": "GAO, commercial space launch insurance and indemnification"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$4de24efb-43ad-5bf2-abf3-b6c103c86c1e$ml$::uuid, $ml$be068b4c-7d4b-59a6-b067-937b837b469a$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$The method was showing its age$ml$, $ml$GAO found the FAA's method dated from 1988 and used a $3 million cost per casualty from 2002 data. A later review suggested $5 to 6 million per casualty.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.gao.gov/products/GAO-12-899", "label": "GAO, commercial space launch insurance and indemnification"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$2eb412fc-2f05-5158-9436-c99d6e07e8a8$ml$::uuid, $ml$be068b4c-7d4b-59a6-b067-937b837b469a$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$The backstop keeps insurance affordable$ml$, $ml$Government cover above the insurance limits what launch companies must buy. Changes to it would raise their costs.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.gao.gov/products/GAO-12-899", "label": "GAO, commercial space launch insurance and indemnification"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$c5c718bd-a97a-54f1-9f38-bb5b04fc87f9$ml$::uuid, $ml$be068b4c-7d4b-59a6-b067-937b837b469a$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In launch, the public is protected by insurance first and taxpayers second.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.gao.gov/products/GAO-12-899", "label": "GAO, commercial space launch insurance and indemnification"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$de8cd675-f2de-59f5-96e6-f0a4d7449dac$ml$::uuid, $ml$c73cbdc4-a090-53fc-b64d-32e3544edfbf$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 26 · The money$ml$, $ml$US launch companies must insure against damage to the public, on average about $99 million per launch. The FAA works out each launch's maximum probable loss and requires insurance for that amount. Above it, the federal government may cover claims up to an extra $1.5 billion in 1988 dollars, about $2.7 billion by 2012, if Congress approves the money. That backstop has never been paid.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.gao.gov/products/GAO-12-899", "label": "GAO, commercial space launch insurance and indemnification"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$8f288ccc-cdeb-572f-b2f3-bff5d6a9c1b2$ml$::uuid, $ml$c73cbdc4-a090-53fc-b64d-32e3544edfbf$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$Layer 1$ml$, $ml$Maximum probable loss is the FAA's estimate of the worst likely damage to others. The launch company must insure up to that amount.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.gao.gov/products/GAO-12-899", "label": "GAO, commercial space launch insurance and indemnification"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$a23073b3-8475-596c-8d75-7cb207f6bada$ml$::uuid, $ml$c73cbdc4-a090-53fc-b64d-32e3544edfbf$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$Layer 2$ml$, $ml$Above the insurance, taxpayers stand behind the launch. Up to about $2.7 billion more, if Congress approves it.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.gao.gov/products/GAO-12-899", "label": "GAO, commercial space launch insurance and indemnification"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$9d1347f0-33ba-5ec3-9a7d-edf09413ff9b$ml$::uuid, $ml$c73cbdc4-a090-53fc-b64d-32e3544edfbf$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$The method was showing its age$ml$, $ml$GAO found the FAA's method dated from 1988 and used a $3 million cost per casualty from 2002 data. A later review suggested $5 to 6 million per casualty.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.gao.gov/products/GAO-12-899", "label": "GAO, commercial space launch insurance and indemnification"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$7556881d-3613-503b-a0a7-9af606987ccd$ml$::uuid, $ml$c73cbdc4-a090-53fc-b64d-32e3544edfbf$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Insurance is a launch cost$ml$, $ml$A launch needs third-party coverage, about $99 million on average. Its premium is part of your launch budget.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.gao.gov/products/GAO-12-899", "label": "GAO, commercial space launch insurance and indemnification"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$3e18890b-8cde-5f2d-838f-6b7e10fab9c6$ml$::uuid, $ml$c73cbdc4-a090-53fc-b64d-32e3544edfbf$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In launch, the public is protected by insurance first and taxpayers second.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.gao.gov/products/GAO-12-899", "label": "GAO, commercial space launch insurance and indemnification"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$e7df79a2-81dc-595a-92d4-3d77aa164bb4$ml$::uuid, $ml$91862eec-f7b5-503c-a947-85b9dc21c811$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 26 · The money$ml$, $ml$US launch companies must insure against damage to the public, on average about $99 million per launch. The FAA works out each launch's maximum probable loss and requires insurance for that amount. Above it, the federal government may cover claims up to an extra $1.5 billion in 1988 dollars, about $2.7 billion by 2012, if Congress approves the money. That backstop has never been paid.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.gao.gov/products/GAO-12-899", "label": "GAO, commercial space launch insurance and indemnification"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$b1ad631b-f6bd-5d7f-b3e3-7134bbfa7f2d$ml$::uuid, $ml$91862eec-f7b5-503c-a947-85b9dc21c811$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$Layer 1$ml$, $ml$Maximum probable loss is the FAA's estimate of the worst likely damage to others. The launch company must insure up to that amount.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.gao.gov/products/GAO-12-899", "label": "GAO, commercial space launch insurance and indemnification"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$786c84f8-bc18-5b8e-8be5-df1da203b4b8$ml$::uuid, $ml$91862eec-f7b5-503c-a947-85b9dc21c811$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$Layer 2$ml$, $ml$Above the insurance, taxpayers stand behind the launch. Up to about $2.7 billion more, if Congress approves it.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.gao.gov/products/GAO-12-899", "label": "GAO, commercial space launch insurance and indemnification"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$be9d51c3-fd44-5ef2-80e7-3c942b50f413$ml$::uuid, $ml$91862eec-f7b5-503c-a947-85b9dc21c811$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$The method was showing its age$ml$, $ml$GAO found the FAA's method dated from 1988 and used a $3 million cost per casualty from 2002 data. A later review suggested $5 to 6 million per casualty.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.gao.gov/products/GAO-12-899", "label": "GAO, commercial space launch insurance and indemnification"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$7054c519-1009-5a94-9a79-e9a64ebad84d$ml$::uuid, $ml$91862eec-f7b5-503c-a947-85b9dc21c811$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Never tested$ml$, $ml$The government backstop for launch accidents dates from 1988 and has never had to pay a claim.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.gao.gov/products/GAO-12-899", "label": "GAO, commercial space launch insurance and indemnification"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$7e8bd141-11e8-57c9-96bc-2f84a2763338$ml$::uuid, $ml$91862eec-f7b5-503c-a947-85b9dc21c811$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In launch, the public is protected by insurance first and taxpayers second.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.gao.gov/products/GAO-12-899", "label": "GAO, commercial space launch insurance and indemnification"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$655064c3-d5e9-5807-ac99-f648aabcc1d1$ml$::uuid, $ml$ae464271-7f3d-5332-903f-4ab4ef37b5a6$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 27 · The power$ml$, $ml$In January 1978, the Soviet satellite Cosmos 954 broke up over Canada. The USSR later paid C$3 million. Under the Outer Space Treaty, a country that launches or procures a launch is internationally liable for damage its space objects cause on Earth, in the air or in space. The settlement with Canada was signed in Moscow in 1981. Because countries carry this liability, they keep tight control over who launches.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/oosa/en/ourwork/spacelaw/nationalspacelaw/bi-multi-lateral-agreements/can_ussr_001.html", "label": "UNOOSA, Canada-USSR Cosmos 954 settlement"}, {"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/spacelaw/activities/2020/SLC2020Presentations/SLC2020PDFPresentations/A._Newman_-_8_Dec_2020_-_Fundamentals_of_Space_Law_.pdf", "label": "UNOOSA, fundamentals of space law"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$6d062549-8513-53c2-8b84-6f1800942ef0$ml$::uuid, $ml$ae464271-7f3d-5332-903f-4ab4ef37b5a6$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$Who is liable$ml$, $ml$Liability follows the launching country, not just the company. It includes the country whose territory the launch leaves from.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/spacelaw/activities/2020/SLC2020Presentations/SLC2020PDFPresentations/A._Newman_-_8_Dec_2020_-_Fundamentals_of_Space_Law_.pdf", "label": "UNOOSA, fundamentals of space law"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$b6edb0bc-de55-5a49-877d-54da083a4526$ml$::uuid, $ml$ae464271-7f3d-5332-903f-4ab4ef37b5a6$ml$::uuid, 3, $ml$Why it matters$ml$, $ml$That liability is why governments hold the power to say no. If they answer for the damage, they decide who flies.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/spacelaw/activities/2020/SLC2020Presentations/SLC2020PDFPresentations/A._Newman_-_8_Dec_2020_-_Fundamentals_of_Space_Law_.pdf", "label": "UNOOSA, fundamentals of space law"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$89fafaa8-a34b-5141-83d3-8932c44bcb58$ml$::uuid, $ml$ae464271-7f3d-5332-903f-4ab4ef37b5a6$ml$::uuid, 4, $ml$Canada accepted a full settlement$ml$, $ml$The 1981 protocol settled all claims from the breakup for C$3 million.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/oosa/en/ourwork/spacelaw/nationalspacelaw/bi-multi-lateral-agreements/can_ussr_001.html", "label": "UNOOSA, Canada-USSR Cosmos 954 settlement"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$908e4057-d889-5834-a711-3594b3482a53$ml$::uuid, $ml$ae464271-7f3d-5332-903f-4ab4ef37b5a6$ml$::uuid, 5, $ml$Know who carries liability$ml$, $ml$Under the treaty, the launching country is liable for damage. That is why regulators move carefully.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/spacelaw/activities/2020/SLC2020Presentations/SLC2020PDFPresentations/A._Newman_-_8_Dec_2020_-_Fundamentals_of_Space_Law_.pdf", "label": "UNOOSA, fundamentals of space law"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$6cae1356-624d-5f20-9e78-7fac278b6eac$ml$::uuid, $ml$ae464271-7f3d-5332-903f-4ab4ef37b5a6$ml$::uuid, 6, $ml$The rule$ml$, $ml$In space, whoever carries the liability holds the power to say no.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/spacelaw/activities/2020/SLC2020Presentations/SLC2020PDFPresentations/A._Newman_-_8_Dec_2020_-_Fundamentals_of_Space_Law_.pdf", "label": "UNOOSA, fundamentals of space law"}, {"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/oosa/en/ourwork/spacelaw/nationalspacelaw/bi-multi-lateral-agreements/can_ussr_001.html", "label": "UNOOSA, Canada-USSR Cosmos 954 settlement"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$29465e86-f58f-54e6-be66-ba78ec8eec4b$ml$::uuid, $ml$3f1b552c-bcd6-580c-8ff1-eff3bce41f5c$ml$::uuid, 1, $ml$Day 27 · The power$ml$, $ml$In January 1978, the Soviet satellite Cosmos 954 broke up over Canada. The USSR later paid C$3 million. Under the Outer Space Treaty, a country that launches or procures a launch is internationally liable for damage its space objects cause on Earth, in the air or in space. The settlement with Canada was signed in Moscow in 1981. Because countries carry this liability, they keep tight control over who launches.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/oosa/en/ourwork/spacelaw/nationalspacelaw/bi-multi-lateral-agreements/can_ussr_001.html", "label": "UNOOSA, Canada-USSR Cosmos 954 settlement"}, {"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/spacelaw/activities/2020/SLC2020Presentations/SLC2020PDFPresentations/A._Newman_-_8_Dec_2020_-_Fundamentals_of_Space_Law_.pdf", "label": "UNOOSA, fundamentals of space law"}]$ml$::jsonb),
($ml$0be3be20-3f51-5564-89f4-49e1455c1bd0$ml$::uuid, $ml$3f1b552c-bcd6-580c-8ff1-eff3bce41f5c$ml$::uuid, 2, $ml$Who is liable$ml$, $ml$Liability follows the launching country, not just the company. It includes the country whose territory the launch leaves from.$ml$, $ml$[{"url": "https://www.unoosa.org/documents/pdf/spacelaw/activities/2020/SLC2020Presentations/SLC2020PDFPresentations/A._Newman_-_8_Dec_2020_-_Fundamentals_of_Space_Law_.pdf", "label": "UNOOSA, fundamentals of space law"}]$ml$::jsonb),
